full_judgment:
Md Abdul Wahhab Miah J :These four leave petitions have arisen out of the judgment and decree dated the 10th day of February, 2011 passed by the High Court Division in Appeal from original Decree Nos.357, 358, 346 and 327 of 2009 dismissing the appeals.

2. Identical facts and common questions of laws being involved, these petitions have been heard together and are disposed of by this single judgment.

3. Facts giving rise to these petitions are as follows:

"Respondent Nos.1-5 of Civil Petition for Leave to Appeal (CP) Nos.2199 of 2011 and 1672 of 2011 as plaintiffs filed Title Suit No.81 of 2006 in the First Court of Joint District Judge, Dhaka impleading the petitioner of CPNo.2199 of 2011, Shamsul Kabir Humayun Reza, son of late Yeadul Hossian of plot No.34(old), 113 (New), B1ock-C WN(B), Gulshan Model Town, Gulshan, Dhaka as defendant No.3 and another Shamsul Kabir Humayun Reza describing him as the son of Md Hossain, subsequently, as son of late Md Yeadul Hossain (in Bengali it was written “পরবর্তীতে পরিবর্তিত) House No.31, Lane No.2, Section-6/A, Mirpur, Dhaka as defendant No.2; MA Hashem, petitioner of CP Nos. 1671 and 1672 of 2011 as defendant No.1 and MNH Bulu as defendant No.4 and Rajdhani Unnayan Kartripakkha (RAJUK), represented by its Chairman and its other officials as defendant Nos.5-8 and Eastern Housing Ltd. as defendant No.9 for the following reliefs:

“ক) ১৫৭ নং বড় মগবাজার নিবাসী প্রকৌশলী সামসুল কবির হুমায়ুন রেজা গত ২-২-২০০৪ ইং তারিখে মৃত্যু বরন করায় তাহার ওয়ারিশ হিসাবে তাহার ওয়ারিশী স্বত্বে বাদীগন নালিশী সম্পত্তির প্রকৃত মালিক মর্মে ১ ও ২ এবং ৩ ও ৪ নং বিবাদী বিরুদ্ধে ঘোষনামূলক ডিক্রী দিতে;

(খ) ১ নং বিবাদীর দাবীকৃত গত ২৯-১২-০২ ইং তারিখের বায়নার দাতা বাসা নং ৩১, লেন নং-০২, সেকশন-৬/এ, মিরপুর নিবাসী ২নং  বিবাদী এবং দেওয়ানী মোকদ্দমা নং-১৭৬/৪ রে বাদী যিনি অত্র মোকদ্দমার ৩ নং বিবাদী উভয় জীবিত রেজা বাদীগনের পিতা নন এবং উক্ত জীবিত সামছুল কবির হুমায়ুন রেজাদ্বয় প্রকৃত পক্ষে ১ ও ৪ নং বিবাদীর সৃজিত অস্তিত্ববিহীন ব্যক্তি মর্মে ঘোষনা মূলক ডিক্রী দিতে;

(গ) ১ নং বিবাদী পক্ষ ২নং বিবাদীপক্ষকে দাতা দেখাইয়া, গত ২৯-১২-২০০২ ইং তারিখে নালিশী সম্পত্তি সম্পর্কে যে বায়নাপত্র সম্পাদন দাবী করিতেছেন এবং ৩নং বিবাদী ৪ নং বিবাদীর বরাবরে গত ১৮-১-২০০৪ ইং তারিখে যে বায়নাপত্র সম্পাদন করিয়াছেন মর্মে দাবী করিতেছেন উক্ত বায়নাপত্র দুইটি পন-প্রবৃত্তিহীন, কাগজি, তঞ্চকী, যোগসাজশী এবং জাল-জালিয়াতি মূলে সৃজিত বিধায় উহা বে-আইনী অকার্যকর এবং বাদীপক্ষের উপর বাধ্যকর নয় মর্মে ঘোষনামূলক ডিক্রী দিতে;

(ঘ) উক্ত গত ২৯-১২-০২ ইং তারিখে উল্লেখে নালিশী সম্পত্তি সম্পর্কে সৃজিত, পন-প্রবৃত্তিহীন, কাগজ, তঞ্চকী, যোগসাজশী এবং জাল-জালিয়াতি বায়নাপত্রের ভিত্তিতে ঢাকার ১ম সাবজজ আদালতে চুক্তি প্রবলের নিমিত্তে ১নং বিবাদীপক্ষ ২নং বিবাদীর বিরুদ্ধে গত ৮-২-০৪ ইং তারিখে  যে দেওয়ানী মোকদ্দমা নং ৫১/০৪ রুজু করিয়াছেন উহা ৩নং বিবাদী বাদী স্বরুপে ৪নং বিবাদীর বরাবরে গত ১৮-১-০৪ ইং তারিখে বায়না সম্পাদন করিয়াছেন মর্মে দাবীতে গত ২৬-৫-০৪ ইং তারিখে ১ম যুগ্ন জেলা জজ আদালতে অপর আরেক ডে দেওয়ানী মোকদ্দমা নং ১৭৬/০৪ রুজু করিয়াছেন উহা যোগসাজাশী ও তঞ্চকী এবং বাদীপক্ষের উপর বাধ্যকর নহে মর্মে অপর আরেকটি ঘোষনামুলক ডিক্রী দিতে;

(ঙ) ১ নং বিবাদী কর্তৃক ২ নং বিবাদীর বিরুদ্ধে ঢাকার ১ম সাবজজ অদালতে চুক্তিপ্রবলের প্রার্থনায় দায়েরকৃত দেওয়ানী মোকদ্দমা নং ৫১/০৪ এবং ৩নং বিবাদীপক্ষ কর্তৃক বিক্রয়ের অনুমতি প্রার্থনায় ৫ হইতে ৮ নং বিবাদীর বিরুদ্ধে দায়েরকৃত ঢাকার ১ম যুগ্ন-জেলা জজ আদালতের দেওয়ানী মোকদ্দমা নং ১৭৬/০৪ যাহাতে ১-৪নং বিবাদীপক্ষ পরিচালনা (sic) করিতে (sic) না পারে এবং নালিশী সম্পত্তিতে বাদীগনের আম-মোক্তার ৯নং বিবাদী ইস্টার্ন হাউজিং লিঃ এর মাধ্যমে বাদীগনের মালিকানা ও শান্তিপূর্ন ভোগ দখলে ১ হইতে ৪ নং বিবাদী পক্ষ যাহাতে কোন প্রকার বাধাবিঘ্ন সৃষ্টি করিতে না পারে তৎমর্মে তাহাদের বিরুদ্ধে চিরস্থায়ী নিষেধাজ্ঞার ডিক্রী দিতে;

(চ) মোকদ্দমা নিস্পত্তি অস্তে বাদীপক্ষ ডিক্রী প্রাপ্ত হইলে ফৌজদারী কার্যবিধি আইনের ৪৭৬ ধারার বিধানমতে ১ নং হইতে ৪ নং বিবাদীকে আসামী করিয়া তাহাদেরকে জাল-জালিয়াতি ও তঞ্চকতামূলে যথাক্রমে গত ২৯-১২-২০০২ ইং ও গত ১৮-১-২০০৪ ইং তারিখে উল্লেখ বায়না সৃজন এবং উক্ত বায়না ব্যবহারের অপরাধে অভিযুক্তক্রমে মাননীয় অত্র আদালতের নিজ স্বাক্ষরে সংশ্লিষ্ট এখতিয়ার সম্পন্ন ফৌজদারী আদালতে অভিযোগ দাখিল করিবার যথাবিহীত ব্যবস্থা গ্রহণ করিতে;

(Prayers ‘ছ’ and ‘জ’ are not quoted those being general prayers).

4. In the plaint, it was stated, inter alia, that Engineer Shamsul Kabir Humayun Reza of 157, Bara Mogbazar was born in village Bardhanpara, police station-Murar-e in the District of Birbhum in West Bengal of the then undivided India. Engineer Shamsul Kabir Humayun Reza of 157, Bara Mogbazar, after passing engineering from Calcutta University in 1950 worked as site Engineer for 2(two) years under Messrs Bridge and Roof Company, India and then migrated to Dhaka and joined C&B Department in 1951 as an Assistant Engineer under the then Government of Pakistan. While serving in C&B department, Engineer Shamsul Kabir Humayun Reza of 157, Bara Mogbazar purchased 10(ten) kathas of land at Bara Mogbazar by a registered deed dated 28- 9-1957 being deed No.9847. Subsequently, he by a registered hebanama dated 17-8-1979 being deed No.31901 transferred the said land of Bara Mogbazar to her wife, late Selima Begum, where- in a three storied building was constructed after obtaining plan from RAJUK. Engineer Humayun Reza resided at 157, Bara Mogbazar till his death on 2-2-2004. As Engineer Shamsul Kabir Humayun Reza was the resident of 157, Bara Mogbazar, in all the Government papers including voter list and passport, his permanent address was mentioned as 157, Bara Mogbazar (hereinafter referred to as Engineer Shamsul Kabir Humayun Reza of Bara Mogbazar). Engineer Shamsul Kabir Humayun Reza of Bara Mogbazar, while serving in C & B, had gone to Canada for taking higher training in 1960 and after completing training, he came back to this Country and again joined C & B and was promoted as the Executive Engineer. At that time, the then Dacca Improvement Trust (DIT) now RAJUK published notice for allotment of plots at Gulshan Model Town for 99 years. Engineer Shamsul Kabir Humayun Reza of Bara Mogbazar applied for allotment of a plot therein and pursuant to his application, he was allotted plot No.34( old), I 13(new), Block-C WN(B) Gulshan Model Town, Gulshan (hereinafter referred to as the suit plot) vide allotment letter under Memo No.2409 dated 21-3-1962. Engineer Humayun Kabir Shamsul Reza of Bara Mogbazar paid the installments against the suit plot on 26-3-1962, 26-6-1962, 7-1-1963, 5-3- 1964 and 3-2-1966. The DIT delivered possession of the suit plot to him by a letter dated 1-8-1962. After getting possession of the allotted plot, Engineer Shamsul Kabir Humayun Reza of Bara Mogbazar created a boundary wall and also constructed a katcha room therein and started to possess the same by keeping a Caretaker. As per the prevailing system at the then DIT, two printed forms of the main lease deed were filled up for keeping one with DIT as lessor and the other for the lessee. The two lease deeds were signed by Abul Ehsan on behalf of the lessor (DIT) and the plaintiffs' father, Engineer Shamsul Kabir Humayun Reza of Bara Mogbazar, the lessee, on 1-8-1962. The serial numbers of two deeds were 7848 and 7849 respectively. Both deeds were registered in the same number being No.7198 and then deed No.7848 was given to the plaintiffs' father and the other deed being serial No.7849 was kept with the other documents in the file of the suit plot at DIT. The plaintiffs' father, Engineer Shamsul Kabir Humayun Reza of Bara Mogbazar, subsequently, had left the job of C & B and joined as Director in one of the company of Islam Group of Zahurul Islam, namely: Bengal Development Corporation. The plaintiffs' father constructed a building measuring 8,000 square feet in every floor (storey has not been mentioned) on the suit plot by obtaining a plan from RAJUK and taking loan from the House Building Finance Corporation by mortgaging the suit plot with it on 17-5-1967. Engineer Shamsul Kabir Humayun Reza of Bara Mogbazar mutated his name with the revenue department of the Government, opened Municipal holding in his name in respect of the suit plot and took connections of various utility services, such as: WASA, Electricity and Titas gas in his own name and paid tax, rents and bills accordingly. He had also let out the said building to different organizations and persons and lastly, it was let out to Aftab Multipurpose Firm Ltd. The names of the tenants are: (1) Agency for International Development, (2) Mr D Markiing and A Lippene ott., (3) Mr LLoye F Gonyea, (4) Mr MM Abouzeid of United Nations Development Programme, (5) Mr Fred HW. Petrini, (6) Sewdish International Service, (7) Dr Martin W. Bloem of Helen Keller International, (8) Uttara Bank Limited and (9) Aftab Bahumukhi Firm Limited.

5. Engineer Shamsul Kabir Humayun Reza of Bara Mogbazar applied to RAJUK on 30-1- 2001 to change the character and nature of the suit plot from residential to commercial plot. RAJUK by its letter dated 14-8-2001 asked Engineer Shamsul Kabir Humayun Reza of Bara Mogbazar to pay Taka 38,28,125 as conversion fee and accordingly, he paid the said amount and the suit plot was converted as a commercial plot from residential one. Thereafter, Engineer Shamsul Kabir Humayun Reza of Bara Mogbazar gave a power of attorney to Eastern Housing Limited, a company of Islam group on 21-12-2003 vide No.15920 dated 21-12-2003 for constructing a commercial building on the suit plot with the power to sell and to do other necessary things. RAJUK duly approved the said power of attorney- Eastern Housing Limited as the attorney of Engineer Shamsul Kabir Humayun Reza of Bara Mogbazar, got a plan passed by RAJUK on 8-2-2003 for constructing a 5th (৬ষ্ঠ তলা) storied building. As per the approved plan, while Engineer Shamsul Kabir Humayun Reza of Bara Mogbazar through his attorney, Eastern Housing Limited, was going to start the construction work in the middle of November, 2003, he got the information that defendant No.1, MA Hashem (petitioner of CP Nos.1671 and 1672 of 2011) in order to grab the suit plot illegally, was engaged in forging papers by using his name (Engineer Shamsul Kabir Humayun Reza) showing his address at House No.31, Lane No.2, Section 6/A, Mirpur, Dhaka. On coming to know about the said information, the plaintiffs' father Engineer Shamsul Kabir Humayun Reza of Bara Mogbazar published a caution notice (সর্তক বিজ্ঞপ্তি) in the daily Janakantha, the Ittefaq, the Observer, the Inquilab and the Protham Alo on 18-11-2003 to the effect that he was the actual owner and was in possession of the suit plot and he entered into a contract with Eastern Housing Limited for constructing a commercial building thereon, and if anyone claimed the suit plot by creating any forged papers using . his name, he would not be responsible by such deed. Long after 1 (one) month of the publication of the said caution notice (notice was published on 18-11-2003) defendant No.1 published a protest notice in the daily Janakantha on 10-12- 2003 that defendant No.2, Shamsul Kabir Humayun Reza, son of Md Hossain of Mirpur was the actual Shamsul Kabir Humayun Reza and he entered into an agreement with him on 29-12- 2002 to sell the suit plot to him and handed over the original documents to him at the time of execution of the agreement. On seeing the said protest notice, the plaintiffs' father became astonished as all the original documents regarding the lease of the suit plot, such as: the letter of allotment, the original lease deed by RAJUK, the original chalans by which installments were paid against the plot, the original letter of delivery of possession of the suit plot, the rent receipts showing payment of rents, mutation with the City Corporation and payment of tax, the lease agreements with the tenants, the bills showing payment of water, electricity and gas, were with him and now with the plaintiffs as his heirs. Father's name of Engineer Shamsul Kabir Humayun Reza of Bara Mogbazar was never Md Hossain, but Yeadul Hossain. In the circumstances, the plaintiffs' father being accompanied by two officers of Eastern Housing Limited, namely: Md Amjadul Haque and Md Emdadul Haque, went to the office of defendant No.1 at Sena Kallyan Bhavan on 10-12-2003 at 4 PM along with the documents mentioned above in respect of the suit plot, his identity card and by showing all those papers tried to convince defendant No.1 that Shamsul Kabir Humayun Reza of Mirpur(supra), son of late Md Hossain with whom he had allegedly entered into a contract to purchase the suit plot, was a fake person and he (the plaintiffs' father) was the actual Shamsul Kabir Humayun Reza and was the owner of the suit plot and was in possession thereof. On hearing the plaintiffs' father Engineer Shamsul Kabir Hurnayun Reza of Bara Moghbazar and seeing his papers in respect of the suit plot and his identity card, defendant NO.1 was sure and certain that the plaintiffs father was the actual Shamsul Kabir Humayun Reza and was the actual owner of the suit plot, he assured him that he would not harass the plaintiffs father by claiming the suit plot on the basis of said agreement. But it was a matter of sorrow that defendant No.1 after coming to know from the plaintiffs father that the actual name of his father was Md Yeadul Hossain not Mohammad Hossain published a corrigendum in the daily Janakantha on 11-12-2003 that father's name of his vendor; Shamsul Kabir Humayun Reza, was Yeadul Hossain, it was wrongly written as Md Hossain in the protest notice published in the daily Janakantha on 10-12-2003.

6. The plaintiffs' father died on 2-2-2004 while living at 157, Bara Moghbazar. As the plaintiffs mother died before the death of their father, they became the 16 annas owner of the suit plot. After the death of the plaintiffs father, they also executed and registered three powers of attorney in favour of Eastern Housing Limited keeping in tact the terms on which their father gave the powers of attorney for constructing the commercial building and Eastern Housing Limited has been possessing the suit plot on behalf of the plaintiffs on the basis of the power of attorney. Since defendant No.1 claimed ownership of the suit plot through defendant No.2 (another Shamsul Kabir Humayun Reza) denying the plaintiffs' father as the actual Shamsul Kabir Humayun Reza, they approached the Ward Commissioner of Ward No.53 for giving a certificate to the effect that they were the heirs of actual Shamsul Kabir Humayun Reza. The Ward Commissioner himself enquired the matter physically on the spot (সরে জমিনে) and found that the plaintiffs were the heirs of actual late Engineer Shamsul Kabir Humayun Reza and issued a certificate of heirs-ship “ওয়ারিশান সার্টিফিকেট” on 16-3-2004. In the mean-time, on 18-3-2004, the plaintiffs through their attorney, Eastern Housing Limited, could know about the filing of Title Suit No.51 of 2004 in the Court of First Joint District Judge, Dhaka by defendant No.1 MA Hashem on 8-2-2004, i.e. 6(six) days after the death of their father for Specific Performance of Contract on the basis of a bainapatra dated 29-12-2002 created by using the name of their father late Engineer Shamsul Kabir Humayun Reza of Bara Moghbazar through a fake person showing his address at House No.31, Lane No.2, Section 6/A, Mirpur (The plaintiffs father died on 2-2-2004) showing the value of the suit plot at Taka 1,65,00,000 (one crore sixty five lakh) only though it was valued at Taka 15,00,00,000 (fifteen crore). On coming to know about the filing of the said suit (Title Suit No.51 of 2004) by defendant No.1, the plaintiffs as the heirs of late Engineer Shamsul KabirHumayun Reza of Bara Moghbazar filed an application on 20-3-2004 under Order I, rule 10 of the Code of Civil Procedure. In the meantime, Mr M Kutubuddin, learned Advocate for the plaintiffs on going through the record of the suit (Title Suit No.51 of 2004) found that the service return of summons of the suit on defendant No.1 (Shamsul Kabir Humayun Reza of Mirpur) had not been filed with the record. In the circumstances, the plaintiffs' learned Advocate enquired the matter in the nejarat and found that summons of the suit had been shown to have been served upon defendant No.1, i. e. fake Shamsul Kabir Humayun Reza of Mirpur on 29-3-2004 by the process server, Mehedi Hassan, in presence of the witnesses mentioned in the service return. However, the learned Advocate for the plaintiffs collected a photostat copy of the service return from the process server, Md Mehedi Hasan, from where it was found that at the time of service of the summons upon defendant No.1, on 29-3-2004 (Shamsul Kabir Humayun Reza of Mirpur), he was shown alive and the summons was received by him by signing in his own hand. The learned Advocate of the plaintiffs approached the learned Advocate for defendant No.1 for giving him, the photostat copies of the documents filed in support of his case, i.e. in Title Suit No.51 of 2004 and then he gave him the photostat copies of the documents including the deed of agreement dated 29-12-2002 and the legal notice; on going through the documents supplied to their learned Advocate, the plaintiffs became sure that those were created fraudulently in a very tactful manner (অত্যন্ত সুকৌশলে). An officer of the plaintiffs' attorney, Eastern Housing Limited went to the address of defendant No.2 of the instant suit, who is defendant No.1 of Title Suit No.51 of 1984, on 25-4-2004 and on enquiry could ascertain that it was a 3(three) storied (তিন তলা) building and the actual owner of the building was one Md Sekander Ali. On query Md Sekander All told that no one named Shamsul Kabir Humayun Reza (defendant No.2), lived in the house. Defendant No.1 (of the instant suit) filed Title Suit No.51 of 2004 for specific performance of contract by creating forged papers in order to grab the suit plot by using the name of the plaintiffs father showing him a resident of Mirpur and got the summons served upon him. There was no existence of the witnesses mentioned in the service return of the summons of defendant No.1. The plaintiffs as the heirs of Engineer Shamsul Kabir Humayun Reza of Bara Moghbazar filed Succession Case No.439 of 2004 in the Court of Joint District Judge, Third Court, Dhaka in order to withdraw the money left behind by their father and they proved that they were the actual heirs of Engineer Shamsul Kabir Humayun Reza Accordingly, succession certificate was issued in their names on 18-5-2004 and they withdrew the money kept by their father in various banks and organizations without any objection from any quarters within the knowledge of defendant Nos.1-4.

7. The learned Joint District Judge, First Court, Dhaka on hearing the application of the plaintiffs dated 20-3-2004 for adding them as defendants in Title Suit No.51 of 2004 by his order dated 15-7-2004 substituted them as the heirs of fake Shamsul Kabir Humayun Reza, i.e, defendant No.1 instead of adding them as the heirs of late Engineer Shamsul Kabir Humayun Reza of Bara Moghbazar although Shamsul Kabir Humayun Reza of Mirpur was alive till then. Against the order dated 15-7-2004 passed by the learned Joint District Judge, in Title Suit No.51 of 2004, the plaintiffs filed Civil Revision No.358 of 2004 before the District Judge, Dhaka. The learned Additional District Judge, Seventh Court, Dhaka who heard the revision application allowed the same and set-aside the order passed by the learned Subordinate Judge and added the plaintiffs as the defendants as the heirs oflate Engineer Shamsul Kabir Humayun Reza of Bara Moghbazar. Challenging the said order of the learned Additional District Judge, the plaintiffs of Title Suit No.51 of 2004 (defendant No.1 of the instant suit) filed Civil Revision No.4608 of 2004 before the High Court Division. The High Court Division by its judgment and order dated 18-7-2005 made the Rule absolute and rejected the application of the plaintiffs for adding them as defendants in their own capacity as the heirs of Engineer Shamsul Kabir Humayun Reza of Bara Moghbazar and thus they remained in the suit as the heirs of fake Shamsul Kabir Humayun Reza of Mirpur as defendant Nos.1 (a)-1(e). The plaintiffs' father late Engineer Shamsul Kabir Humayun Reza was the resident of 157, Bara Moghbazar and he always resided there permanently and he never resided at House No.31, Lane No.2, Section-6/A, Mirpur or in any other house and even not in the suit plot. Shamsul Kabir Humayun Reza, i.e. defendant No.2 of the instant suit (defendant No.1 in Title Suit No.51 of 2004) was an imaginary person created by defendant NO.1 of the instant suit. Defendant No.1 in order to grab the suit plot through fraud and forgery created the bainapatra on 29-12-2002 showing defendant No.2 of the instant suit as his vendor. After obtaining the certified copy of the judgment of Civil Revision No.4608 of 2004, on 28-11-2005, Md Amjadul Haque, an officer of the plaintiff’s attorney, Eastern Housing Limited met defendant No.1 again and requested him either to withdraw Title Suit No.51 of 2004 filed by him or not to prose- cute the same, but he refused to concede to the request and further told that he would prosecute the suit. On 26-2-2006, when Emdadul Haque, another employee of the plaintiffs' attorney went to RAJUK's office to enquire about the file of the suit plot, its Estate Officer handed over him the photostat copy of the plaint of Title Suit No.176 of 2004 of the same Court, i.e. the First Joint District Judge and the documents filed therewith (the suit was filed on 26-2-2006) and informed him that another fake Shamsul Kabir Humayun Reza filed the said suit showing his address at the suit plot claiming to be its owner. The suit was filed as he failed to obtain permission from RAJUK to sell the suit plot to defendant No.4 on the basis of an agreement allegedly entered by him with defendant No.4 on 18-1-2004. Defendant Nos.2 and 3 are fake Shamsul Kabir Humayun Reza and none of them was the owner of the suit plot and was never in possession thereof and the bainapatras created by them were collusive, ineffective, illegal and were mere paper transactions. The bainapatras created by the said defendants were not binding upon the plaintiffs or their father late Engineer Shamsul Kabir Humayun Reza of Bara Moghbazar. The creation of two bainapatras by two fake Shamsul Kabir Humayun Reza and the other documents created cloud over the plaintiffs' title in the suit plot and also their parentage for which they were compelled to file the suit. The agreement dated 29-12-2002 being a forged one was not binding upon the plaintiffs and defendant No.1 was liable to be proceeded with under section 476 of the Code of Criminal Procedure after disposal of the suit for using the forged agreement in Court.

8. Defendant No.1, MA Hashem (hereinafter referred to as the defendant) contested the suit by filing a written statement denying the material statements made in the plaint and con- tending, inter alia, that the suit was not maintain- able in its present form. The plaintiffs had no cause of action to file the suit and the same was also barred by limitation. The plaintiffs' father got allotment of the suit plot from the then DIT now RAJUK on 2-3-1962 vide Memo No.DIT/ Gulshan/2409. Lease deed was executed and registered on 1-8-1962 being deed No.7194 and possession of the suit plot was delivered to him. The plaintiffs' father remained in possession of the suit plot by mutating-his name and paying rents to the Government and also by opening holding number with the Dhaka City Corporation. The plaintiffs' father created a temporary structure on the suit plot and while he had been in possession thereof, he entered into an agreement with the defendant on 29-12-2002 for selling the same at Taka 1,65,00,000 and on receipt of a sum of Taka 35,00,000 in presence of the witness on the same date handed over the original deed of the suit plot to him on the condition that after obtaining per- mission from RAJUK and the other authorities, he would register the deed on receipt of the balance consideration. It was further agreed that the plaintiffs' father would pay the gain tax, stamp duty, registration fees and other expenses necessary for registration of the deed. RAJUK was informed about the said bainapatra on 6-1-2003. The defendant by a letter dated 1-8-2003 requested the plaintiffs' father to register the kabala quickly obtaining permission from RAJUK which was received by him on 3-8-2003. The plaintiffs' father by a letter dated 11-8-2003 informed the defendant that he could not take step for registration of the kabala for various physical and mental problems. He also requested the defendant to pay Taka 5,00,000 more and accordingly, the defendant paid him Taka 3,20,000 by a cheque dated 12-8-2003 which was duly encashed. But in spite of receipt of the said money (Taka 3,20,000), the plaintiffs' father did not take any step for permission from RAJUK to sell the suit plot. As per terms of the agreement, the plaintiffs' father was supposed to register the deed before 28-12-2003 on receipt of the balance consideration, but he did not do so. In the circumstances, the defendant requested the plaintiffs' father on 29-12-2002 in presence of the witnesses to register the deed on receipt of the balance money, but he refused to register the deed. Thereafter, on 1-12-2003, the defendant through his lawyer sent a legal notice to the plaintiffs' father requesting him to execute and register the sale deed taking all necessary steps in the matter and on receipt of the balance consideration of Taka 1,26,80,000 which was duly received by him on 3-12-2003. On 8-12-2003, the defendant could know that one Engineer Shamsul Kabir Humayun Reza in a notice dated 18-11- 2003 published in the daily Janakantha stated that he did not enter into any contract with anyone for selling the suit plot and that he started construction work therein by obtaining a plan from RAJUK through a contract entered into with Eastern Housing Limited. The defendant did not know about the notice published by Engineer Shamsul Kabir Humayun Reza in the daily Janakantha dated 18-11-2003. However, subsequently, the defendant published a protest notice in the daily Janakantha on 10-12-2003 denying the contents of the notice dated 18-11-2003. But after publication of the protest notice, it was found that father's name of Shamsul Kabir Humayun Reza was mistakenly, (ভুল বশতঃ) written as only Md Hossain in place of Md Yeadul Hossain for which letter was written to the daily Janakantha to publish the father's name of Shamsul Kabir Humayun Reza correctly and accordingly, on 11- 12-2003, corrigendum of the protest was published stating the father's name of Shamsul Kabir Humayun Reza as Md Yeadul Hossain. After publication of the protest notice, the defendant went to RAJUK and on seeing the record found that a plan was passed by it, but name and address of the person was not available in the record. Since the plaintiffs are the legal heirs of Shamsul Kabir Humayun Reza, they were bound by the terms of the bainapatra executed by their father. After the death of the plaintiffs' father, on 2-2-2004, they filed an application in Title Suit No.51 of 2004 on 20-3-2004 for adding them as defendants in the suit. The defendant who was the plaintiff of Title Suit No.51 of2004 also filed a counter application in the suit supporting the application of the present plaintiffs to add them as defendants in the suit. The application was allowed on 15-7-2004. Against the said order, the plaintiffs (of the instant suit) filed Civil Revision No.358 of 2004 before the District Judge, Dhaka which was allowed. Against the said order allowing the civil revision, the defendant filed Civil Revision No.4608 of 2004 before the High Court Division. The Rule in the civil revision was made absolute and the judgment and order passed by the Additional. District Judge, Seventh Court, Dhaka was set-aside and thus, the plaintiffs remained substituted as defendant Nos.1(a)-1e) in place of defendant No.1 and got chance to contest Title Suit No.51 of 2004, so there was no necessity on the part of the plaintiffs to file the instant suit.

9. Defendant No.3, another Shamsul Kabir Humayun Reza, who also claimed to be the son of late Yeadul Hossain also filed a written statement to contest the suit. In his written statement, it was contended, inter alia, that the plaintiffs had no cause of action to file the suit and the suit was not maintainable in its present form. The suit was also barred by limitation. Defendant No.3 joined the then C & B department under the Ministry of Communications in 1952 as an Assistant Engineer and in 1960 was appointed as Executive Engineer (Design). While in service, the defendant applied to the then DIT now RAJUK to allot him a plot at Gulshan and on his application, he was allotted the suit plot vide allotment letter No.2409 dated 21-3-1962. After the defendant paid the price of the suit plot by installments, possession thereof was delivered to him and then the standard lease deed was executed and registered in his favour on 1-8-1962 being deed No.7198 and thus the defendant became the sixteen annas owner of the suit plot. Defendant No.3 mutated his name with the Government record, City Corporation and also recorded his name with the various utility services authorities, such as: electricity, WASA, Titas gas in respect of the suit plot and has been in possession thereof by paying rents, taxes and bills. In the last RS record and Metropolitan Survey, the suit plot was correctly recorded in the name of defendant No.3. The defendant applied to RAJUK on 25-1-1996 for permission to construct building on the suit plot and accordingly, RAJUK gave him permission to construct a two storied building vide Memo No.4452 dated 29-10-1996. Defendant No.3 constructed a two storied building on the suit plot and started living there. He being in urgent need of money entered into a contract for selling the suit plot with defendant No.4, MNH. Bulu at Taka 2,35,00,000 (two crore thirty five lakh) and on receipt of a sum of Taka 5,00,000 as advance executed a bainapatra in his favour. The defendant applied to RAJUK on 18-2- 2004 for sale permission of the suit plot, but RAJUK did not pay any heed to his said prayer. He sent four legal notices to defendant Nos.5- 8(RAJUK and its officials) separately on 20-4- 2004 which they received on 22-4-2004, but they did not reply to the notices. In the circumstances, the defendant was compelled to file Title Suit No.176 of 2004 in the same Court on 31-5-2004. On receipt of the summons of the said suit, the defendants appeared therein on 28-8-2004, but did not file any written statement for. a long time and when the suit was fixed for ex-parte hearing suddenly on 8-3-2006, the defendants have filed written statement and are contesting the suit which is still pending for hearing. Defendant No.3 applied to the Parliamentary Standing Committee of the Ministry of Housing and Works for giving back possession of his house (the suit plot). On such application, a Sub-Committee was formed with Joynal Abedin Farooqui as its Convener and Chairman, RAJUK as its member Secretary and they were requested to take necessary steps in the matter. The Sub-Committee decided to hold a Commission in RAJUK's Building on 25-7-2004, but due to the terrorist activities of the plaintiffs, the next date was fixed on 28-7-2004 (Title Suit No.176 of 2004 was filed by the defendant on 31- 5-2004). On the date, defendant No.3 appeared before the Sub-Committee and gave his statement, but none appeared on behalf of the plaintiffs and defendant No. I. After hearing the Commission decided that since defendant No.3 was a government servant, RAJUK would collect particulars as to his job from the concerned Department and then the Standing Committee would be informed about the decision of the sub-commit- tee. On 16-9-2004, the Secretary, RAJUK requested the concerned section of the Ministry of Communications to send the information in respect of the service of defendant No.3. The Superintendent Engineer, Accounts Department (স ও জ) of the Ministry of Communications sent information on 5-3-2005 from where it appeared that defendant No.3, Shamsul Kabir Humayun Reza joined the Design Department-1 of C & B in project circle No.2 as Assistant Engineer on 19-8- 1952 and was promoted to the post of Executive Engineer (Design Department) and Building project circle on 19-7-1960. From the report, it further appeared that the accounts maintained with the Department showed that defendant NO.3 had withdrawn his salary and other allowances upto 18-8-1963. In the report, the address of defendant No.3 was shown at T-75, Railway Colony, Babupara, Sub-Division, Ishwardy. The report proved that the defendant was the actual Shamsul Kabir Humayun Reza and the plaintiffs' father, Shamsul Kabir Humayun Reza, was a fake person. The plaintiffs and defendant No.1 both claiming to be the heirs and “বায়না গৃহিতা” respectively of Shamsul Kabir Humayun Reza were conspiring to grab the property of defendant No.3, actual Shamsul Kabir Humayun Reza by resorting to forgery. The defendant paid the lease money to RAJUK and he was in his possession of the original letter of allotment of the suit plot, the receipts of the payment of installments against the suit plot, the letter of delivering possession of the suit plot, the original lease deed, the permission letter to undertake pucca construction on the suit plot, the approved plan and design. If the plaintiffs or their attorney filed any of the above-mentioned documents, those would be false and forged. The plaintiffs had no right, title and possession in the suit plot and it is the defendant who has been possessing the suit plot as the lessee of RAJUK himself and through his men. The plaintiffs, being instigated (প্ররোচিত) and influenced  (প্রভাবিত) by their attorney, Eastern Housing Limited, created some false and forged papers showing a fake Shamsul Kabir Humayun Reza as their father and then in collusion with defendant Nos.5-8 filed the false suit as a test case just to harass and cause financial loss to defendant No.3, actual Shamsul Kabir Humayun Reza.

10. Defendant Nos.5-8 (RAJUK and its officials) in their written statement contended, inter alia, that the suit plot was allotted to Engineer Shamsul Kabir Humayun Reza, the predecessor- in-interest of the plaintiffs by the then DIT and after he paid the installments against the plot, possession thereof was delivered to him. And then the lease deed was executed and registered on 1-8-1962 being deed No.7198 and the lessee after getting possession thereof took water, electricity and gas connection therein. The lessee also mutated his name in the concerned tahsil office and municipality and paid rents, taxes and other utility service bills. While the plaintiffs' predecessor-in-interest had been in possession of the suit plot, he applied to RAJUK for approving a plan and accordingly, a plan was approved. Then he constructed the building on the suit plot and remained in possession thereof through 'bharatias'. Subsequently, Engineer Shamsul Kabir Humayun Reza entered into an agreement with Eastern Housing Limited for making construction of a multistoried building on the suit plot after obtaining due permission from RAJUK and also appointed Eastern Housing Limited as his attorney by executing and registering a power of attorney on 21-12-2003 being No.15942 and delivered possession of the suit plot to it. Engineer Shamsul Kabir Humayun Reza died on 2-2-2004 while residing at 157, Bara Moghbazar owned by his wife leaving behind the plaintiffs as his two sons and 3(three) daughters and their names have been duly mutated with RAJUK as the lessees. Neither the original lessee, Engineer Shamsul Kabir Humayun Reza nor after his death, the plaintiff sentered into any contract with MNH Bulu or anybody else for selling the suit plot and there was no proof of filing any such paper with RAJUK. MNH Bulu in order to grab the suit plot created an imaginary story of entering into an agreement with a fake Shamsul Kabir Humayun Reza as a lessee of RAJUK long after the death of Engineer Shamsul Kabir Humayun Reza on 2-2-2004 and got Title Suit No.176 of 2004 filed through him for getting permission to sell the suit plot to him. RAJUK has been contesting Title Suit No.176 of 2004 by filing written statement denying the statements made in the plaint and stating the actual facts. One MA Hashem, son of late Alhaj Abdul Aziz also filed Title Suit No.51 of 2004 in the same Court for specific performance of contract on the basis of a bainapatra impleading one Shamsul Kabir Humayun Reza as a lessee and others as defendants to grab the suit plot and RAJUK as defendant No.3 has been contesting the suit by filing written statement denying the material statements made in the plaint and stating actual facts. There was no existence of Shamsul Kabir Humayun Reza as claimed in the plaint of Title Suit Nos.176 of 2004 and 510f 2004 with the record of RAJUK and Engineer Shamsul Kabir Humayun Reza, the predecessor-in-interest of the plaintiffs was the valid lessee of the suit plot and after his death on 2-2-2004, the same has been mutated in the name of the plaintiffs as its owners in the records of RAJUK and, in fact, they are the actual owners and are in possession thereof. RAJUK is the actual owner of the suit plot. No application was filed to RAJUK by the actual lessee for permission to sell the suit plot either to MNH Bulu of Title Suit No. 176 of 2004 or MA Hashem of Title Suit No.51 of 2004.

11. The petitioner of CP No.1671 of 2011 who was defendant No.1 in Title Suit No:81 of 2006 filed Title Suit No.51 of 2004 in the same Court (Joint District Judge, First Court, Dhaka) for specific performance of contract impleading Shamsul Kabir Humayun Reza of House No.31, Lane No.2, Section 6/A, Mirpur as principal defendant No.1 and one Mr Akhtaruzzaman, Director Eastern Housing Limited as principal defendant No.2, the Chairman, RAJUK and the People's Republic of Bangladesh, represented by the Deputy Commissioner, Dhaka as pro-forma defendant Nos.3 and 4 (although in law there is nothing as principal or pro-forma defendants).

12. In the plaint, it was stated, inter alia, that the then DIT now RAJUK allotted the suit plot to defendant No.1 vide allotment letter No.DIT/ Gulshan /2409 dated 2-3-1962 and the lease deed was registered between RAJUK and defendant No.1 on 1-8-1962 being deed No.7198 and possession of the suit plot was delivered to him. Defendant No.1 mutated the suit plot in his name and has been possessing the same in his own right, title and interest. Defendant No.1 constructed some temporary structures on the suit plot and paid rents and taxes regularly to the Government and also opened separate holding with Dhaka City Corporation. Defendant No.1 entered into an agreement with the plaintiff on 29-12-2002 for sale of the suit plot at a consideration of Taka' 1,65,00,000 (Taka one crore sixty five lakh) only and a deed of agreement for sale was signed and executed on the said date on receiving Taka 35,00,000 only in cash as advance money. Defendant No.1 handed over all original documents to the plaintiff in presence of the witnesses. In the agreement, it was stipulated that defendant No.1 as the seller "shall apply for and obtain all necessary permission/clearance certificate" from RAJUK and to the other relevant authorities for sale of the suit plot in favour of the plaintiff and that the plaintiff will be responsible for payment of transfer fee to RAJUK, gain tax, stamp duty, registration fee and all other charges and incidental costs and expenses and the execution and registration of the sale deed by defendant No.1 shall be completed within one year and that the balance amount of Taka 1,30,00,000 only will be received by defendant No.1 from the plaintiff at the time of registration of the sale deed and that upon failure of defendant NO.1 to execute and register the deed of sale in favour of the plaintiff, he shall be entitled to get the sale deed executed and registered through Court. The plaintiff informed RAJUK through its Deputy Director (Estate) by letter dated 6-1-2003 about transfer of the suit plot by defendant No.1 in his favour on the basis of the said deed of agreement for sale. The plaintiff by letter dated 1-8-2003 requested defendant NO.1 to take immediate step for permission from RAJUK for registering the deed of sale in his favour. In response to the letter dated 1-8-2003, defendant No.1 informed the plaintiff by letter dated 11-8- 2003 that due to physical and mental difficulties, he could not take appropriate steps for registration of the sale deed. By the said letter (11-8-2003), defendant No.1 asked the plaintiff to make further payment of Taka 5,00,000 (Taka five lakh) only against the said deed of agreement with the assurance that he would take appropriate step immediately and would also inform the plaintiff very soon thereabout. The plaintiff paid a further sum of Taka 3,20,000 (Taka three lakh twenty thousand) only to defendant No.1 vide cheque No.1279703 dated 12-8-2003 drawn on AB Bank Ltd. Principal Branch, Dhaka, the cheque was given through a covering letter dated 12-8-2008 wherein request was made by the plaintiff to the defendant to take all necessary steps immediately so that he could register the deed of sale in favour of the plaintiff within the schedule time, that is, by 28-12-2003. Defendant No.1 was further requested to inform the plaintiff about the steps taken by him in that regard. The cheque was duly encashed by defendant No.1, but unfortunately until 1st December, 2003, he did not inform the plaintiff anything about the steps taken by him for registration of the deed of sale especially with regard to obtaining sale permission from RAJUK. In the circumstances, the plaintiff issued a notice on 1-1-2003 to defendant NO.1 through his lawyer requesting him to complete all formalities and obtain permission from RAJUK and to execute and register the deed of sale in his favour by the 20th day of December, 2003 and to receive the balance amount of Taka 1,26,80,000 only from him. In spite of the above requests and persuasions of the plaintiff, defendant No.1 failed to execute and register the deed of sale in favour of the plaintiff by 28-12-2003. Lastly on 29-12-2003, the plain- tiff in presence of the witnesses offered the balance amount to defendant No.1 and requested him to execute and register the sale deed, but the defendant refused to do so. The plaintiff to his utter shock and surprise learnt "on or about 8th December, 2003" about publication of a (বিজ্ঞপ্তি) in the daily "Janakantha", i.e. after issuance of the legal notice dated 1-12-2003 published by one Engineer Shamsul Kabir Humayun Reza that he was the owner of the suit plot and he had entered into a contract with Eastern Housing Limited for constructing a building thereon and on the basis of the contract Eastern Housing Limited had already started all the works in that respect after approval of the plan by RAJUK. Thus, the plaintiff hunched a foul play in the matter for which he was compelled to publish a protest notice (বিজ্ঞপ্তি) in the daily Janakantha on 10-12-2003 protesting the aforementioned notice (বিজ্ঞপ্তি) dated 18-11-2003 and stating that the contents of the notice dated 18-11-2003 were false, imaginary and fraudulent as he became the owner of the suit plot on the basis of bainapatra entered into with him by its owner Shamsul Kabir Humayun Reza, son of Md Hossain and all the original documents relating to the suit plot were with him. In the protest notice, it was further stated that the fact of entering into contract by Shamsul Kabir Humayun Reza, original owner, was intimated to RAJUK by a letter dated 6-1-2003 which was received by RAJUK on 7-1-2003. As some mistakes occurred in the protest notice dated 10-12-2003 as to the father's name of Md Shamsul Kabir Humayun Reza, a corrigendum was issued stating his father's name as Md Yeadul Hossain in place of Md Hossain. Thereafter, the plaintiff could manage to look into the records of RAJUK and found his application dated 5-1-2003 was in the file and further learnt that a plan had been approved by RAJUK IMc construction on the suit plot, but the plaintiff could not collect the exact name and address of the person in whose favour or upon whose request, the alleged plan was approved by RAJUK. There had been no protest from any quarter against the protest notice issued by the plaintiff in the daily Janakantha on 10-12-2003. The plaintiff went to the suit plot on 22-12-2003 and found that some people were taking steps for construction removing the old structures and on query he learnt that they were the employees/workers of defendant No.2 who was claimed by them to be the owner of the suit plot and on hearing the same, the plaintiff Was further surprised and puzzled. The failure/ inaction/refusal of defendant No.1 to execute and register the sale deed in favour of the plaintiff in spite of the request and legal notice mentioned hereinabove and also in applying for and in obtaining permission for sale of the suit plot in favour of the plaintiff in spite of his readiness and willingness to perform his obligation under the deed of agreement compelled the plaintiff to file the suit for specific performance of contract.

13. The suit was contested by the heirs of Engineer Shamsul Kabir Humayun Reza of 157, Bara Moghbazar, i.e. the plaintiffs of Title Suit No.81 of 2006 as defendant Nos.1(a)-1(e) by filing written statement denying the material allegations made in the plaint and stating, inter alia, the facts as stated in the plaint of Title Suit No.81 of 2006 as have been stated hereinbefore. Therefore, we do not consider it necessary to repeat the same here. However, it is pertinent to state that the heirs of Engineer Shamsul Kabir Humayun Reza of Bara Moghbazar were substituted as defendant Nos. 1(a)-1(e) at the instance of the plaintiff, i.e. MA Hashem although they applied to be added as defendants in the suit as the heirs of Engineer Shamsul Kabir Humayun Reza in their own independent capacity.

14. One Shamsul Kabir Humayun Reza claiming himself to be the son of late Md Yeadul Hossain showing his address at the suit plot, filed Title Suit No.176 of 2004 in the same Court of the Joint District Judge, (1st Court, Dhaka) impleading: (a) the Chairman, RAJUK (b) the Member (Estate) RAJUK, (c) the Director (Estate) RAJUK and (d) the Deputy Director (Estate) RAJUK as defendants for declaration that he was legally entitled to get sale permission and the defendants were also bound to issue sale permission in respect of the suit plot "by receiving the proper transfer fees" from him as per the rules of RAJUK asserting the facts as stated in the written statement filed by him in Title Suit No.81 of 2006 as defendant No.3 which have been stated hereinbefore. Therefore, those are not repeated herein.

15. The suit was contested by the defendants by filing written statement denying the material statements made in the plaint and contending, inter alia, the facts as stated in the written statement filed in Title Suit No.81 of2006. In the written statement additional facts stated were that the plaintiff by posing himself as Engineer Shamsul Kabir Humayun Reza filed the suit to grab the suit plot. So proceeding should be drawn against him under section 476(1) of the Code of Criminal Procedure for filing the suit by creating forged document. Value of the suit plot was about Taka 10 crore, whereas the plaintiff, fake Shamsul Kabir Humayun Reza created a forged bainapatra showing the value of the suit plot at Taka 2,35,00,000 only. The original lessee of RAJUK did not file any application to it on 18-2-2004 or on any other day for permission to sell the suit plot to the person mentioned in the plaint and no such application was also available with the record of RAJUK, therefore, no declaration as prayed for could be given from the Court. Plaintiff, fake Shamsul Kabir Humayun Reza of the instant suit neither got any allotment of the suit plot from RAJUK nor got possession thereof nor got any lease deed. The original lessee died on 2-2-2004. Plaintiff, fake Shamsul Kabir Humayun Reza had no right, title and interest in the suit plot and, as such, he was not entitled to get any relief in the suit and the same was liable to be dismissed.

16. All the three suits were disposed of by the learned Joint District Judge, First Court, Dhaka on the same date, i.e. on 30-7-2009 by separate judgment and decree. Of the three suits, Title Suit No.81 of 2006 was decreed declaring the plaintiffs title in the suit plot as the heirs of Engineer Shamsul Kabir Humayun Reza of 157, Bara Moghbazar. The learned Joint District Judge also restrained the defendants permanently from creating any obstacle in the title and possession of the plaintiffs in the suit plot. The learned Joint District Judge dismissed Title Suit Nos.51 of2004 and 176 of 2004.

17. Being aggrieved by and dissatisfied with the judgment and decree passed by the learned Joint District Judge in Title Suit NO.51 of 2004, plaintiff-MA Hashem filed Appeal from Original Decree (First Appeal) No.357 of 2009 before the High Court Division. Against the judgment and decree passed in Title Suit No.81 of 2006, MA Hashem, defendant No.1, filed Appeal from Original Decree (First Appeal) No.358 of 2009. Against the judgment and decree passed in Title Suit No.81 of. 2006, defendant No.3-Shamsul Kabir Humayun Reza, i.e. the plaintiff of Title Suit No.176 of 2004, filed Appeal from Original Decree (First Appeal) No.346 of2009 and against the judgment and decree passed in Title Suit No.176 of 2004, he also filed Appeal from Original Decree (First Appeal). No.327 of 2009 A Division Bench of the High Court Division hearing the four appeals analogously by the impugned judgment dismissed all of them.

18. Against the judgment and decree passed in First Appeal Nos.346, 357, 358 and 327 of 2009, the respective petitioner has filed these four leave petitions.

19. Mr Abdul Wadud Bhuiyan, learned Advocate, appearing for the petitioner in CP No.2199 of 2009, Mr Rokanuddin Mahmud, learned Advocate, appearing for the petitioner in CPNo.1672 of2009, Mr AF Hassan Ariff, learned Advocate, appearing for the petitioner in CP No.2198 of 2009 and Mr Fida M Kamal, learned Advocate, appearing for the petitioner in CP No.1671 of2009, have made common submission that the learned presiding Judge of the Division Bench proceeded to dispose of the appeals with the adverse view as to documents produced by the respective petitioner before considering the respective case with reference to the evidence on record as is apparent from the observation made by him at the beginning of the judgment to the effect: "The cases before me are unique cases of forgery. Allottees, 3 in number are of same name, same parentage, similar hand writings with similar apparently same documents but 3 persons claiming the plot of RAJUK of them one must be genuine with original papers and others are not. I have taken a strong heavy burden to single out that genuine person with original documents." and because of such adverse view, due consideration to the evidence produced by the respective petitioner, both oral and documentary, was not given in deciding the merit of his claim to the suit plot and thus, they have been seriously prejudiced. The learned Advocates have further submitted that in the facts and circumstances of the case, particularly, of the fact that three sets of Shamsul Kabir Humayun Reza surfaced in the scene with three sets of original documents which included the letter of allotment, receipts showing payment of installments against the suit plot and the lease deed, it was incumbent upon the trial Court to frame an issue as to which sets of documents, were genuine and who was the actual Shamsul Kabir Humayun Reza and then to decide the issue by calling the originals of those documents from RAJUK, but the trial Court neither framed any issue nor decided the matter on verification ofthe originals kept with RAJUK and thus, erred in law in decreeing Title Suit No.81 of2006 while dismissing the two other suits' and that the High Court Division being the last Court of fact could also call for the originals from RAJUK and decide the above points, but it did not do and decided the merit of the appeals on the documents produced by the parties concurring with those of the trial Court and thus fell into an error in passing the impugned judgment and decree. The learned Advocates have made prayer for sending the suit back on remand to the trial Court for ascertaining the genuineness of three sets of documents produced by the parties and also to ascertain which Shamsul Kabir Humayun Reza was the actual allotted of the suit plot. In the alternative, they have also prayed that the appeals may be sent back to the High Court Division for decision afresh to decide the above points raised by them. The learned Advocates have also made a common submission that the trial Court erred in law in adopting the depositions of the PWs examined in Title Suit Nos.51 of 2004, 176 of 2004 and 81 of 2006 as evidence in support of the respective case as made out in the respective written statement filed in the respective suit in violation of the pro- visions of section 33 of the Evidence Act and that although the said point was agitated before the High Court Division, it discarded the same on misconception of the provisions of section 33 thereof. On this point, Mr Bhuiyan relied upon the cases of Ali Haider vs State, 10 DLR (SC) 193 and Allah Ditta vs State, 11 DLR (SC) 38.

20. Mr Abdul Wadud Bhuiyan has further submitted that the documents filed by the petitioner (CP No.2199 of 2011) as plaintiff in Title Suit No.176 of 2004 (he was defendant No.3 in Title Suit No.81 of 2006) were the original documents regarding the lease of the suit plot and those conclusively proved that he was the actual Shamsul Kabir Humayun Reza and allottee of the suit plot, the trial Court was wrong in decreeing Title Suit No.81 of 2006 on the view that the plaintiffs (Title Suit No.81 of 2006) proved that the suit plot was allotted to Shamsul Kabir HumayunReza of 157, Bara Moghbazar and the High Court Division also being the last Court of fact failed to consider the documents filed by him with reference to his case as made out in his written statement filed in the suit and thus fen into an error in affirming the judgment and decree passed by the trial Court.

21. Mr 'Fida M Kamal appearing in CP No.1671 of 2011 has submitted that petitioner MA Hashem as plaintiff of Title Suit No.51 of 2004 proved conclusively that Shamsul Kabir Humayun Reza of 157, Bara Moghbazar, i.e. father of defendant Nos.1(a)-1(c) entered into an agreement with him for selling the suit plot at a consideration of Taka 16,50,00,000 and accordingly, executed the bainapatra on 29-12-2002 on receipt of Taka 35,00,000 as advance and the sub- sequent payment of Taka 3,20,000 and his willingness to pay the balance amount and thus fulfilled all the requirements within the meaning of section 12 of the Specific Relief Act, the trial Court as wen as the High Court Division erred in law in dismissing the suit. Mr Fida M Kamal has further submitted that from order No.5 dated 20- 3-2004, it was clear that after the death of defendant No.1, Engineer Shamsul Kabir Humayun Reza, the plaintiff filed an application for substituting his heirs and ultimately, the said application was allowed and the heirs of defendant No.1 were substituted on 15-7-2004 by order No. 13, the High Court Division was wrong in observing that from 8-2-2004 to 15-7-2004, the plaintiff did not take any step, in the matter either for substituting or adding the heirs of Shamsul Kabir Humayun Reza of 157, Bara Moghbazar in the suit and thus, fell into an error in deciding the genuineness of the agreement dated 29-12-2002 on the view that the plaintiff did not accept Shamsul Kabir Humayun Reza as his vendor. Mr Fida M Kamal has further submitted that the evidence on record proved conclusively that Shamsul Kabir Humayun Reza of Mirpur and of 157, Bara Moghbazar was the same and similar person, who entered into an agreement with the plaintiff on 29-12-2002 for selling the suit plot, but the High Court Division on misreading and on mis-appreciation of the evidence on record erroneously held that Shamsul Kabir Humayun Reza of Mirpur and Bara Moghbazar were two different persons and that Shamsul Kabir Humayun Reza of Bara Moghbazar did not enter into any agreement for sale of the suit plot with the plaintiff and thus, erred in law in dismissing the suit.

22. Mr Rokanuddin Mahmud, appearing for MA Hashem inCPNo.1672 0f 2011, has submitted that Title Suit No.81 of 2006 was not maintainable in law as prior to the filing of that suit, the petitioner (defendant No.1 of Title Suit No.81 of 2006) had already filed Title Suit No.51 of 2004 for specific performance of contract against the plaintiffs predecessor, Shamsul Kabir Humayun Reza and in that suit, they were substituted as defendant Nos.1(a)-1(e) and thus, they had the scope to prove their case, but the High Court Division failed to consider this factual and legal aspect of the case in affirming the judgment and decree passed by the trial Court. He has further submitted that a suit for specific performance of contract cannot be converted into a title suit, but, in fact, the heirs of Shamsul Kabir Humayun Reza by filing Title Suit No.81 of 2006 for declaration that the agreement for sale in favour of defendant No.1 and another agreement in favour of defendant No.4 were false, forged and not binding upon them and also for declaration of their title and for permanent injunction, the suit was converted into a title suit. Therefore, the trial Court ought to have dismissed Title Suit No.81 of 2006 as being not maintainable; the High Court Division failed to apply its judicial mind to consider this legal aspect of the case as well and thus erred in law in dismissing the appeal affirming those of the trial Court.

23. Mr AF Hassan Ariff appearing in CP No.2198 of 2011 separately argued that plaintiff, Shamsul Kabir Humayun Reza proved conclusively that he was the actual Shamsul Kabir Humayun Reza and the actual owner of the suit plot and, as such, he had every right to dispose of his property by way of sale and since RAJUK did not give permission to sell the same in spite of prayer made by him, he had no other alternative but to file the suit. But the High Court Division, on an erroneous approach to the case and also on misreading of the evidence on record, found that the plaintiff was not the real owner of the suit plot and thus dismissed the suit. He has further submitted that the trial Court as well as the High Court Division erred in law in holding that Shamsul Kabir Humayun Reza of Bara Moghbazar was the actual Shamsul Kabir Humayun Reza and the owner of the suit plot ignoring the fact that the plaintiff Shamsul Kabir Humayun Reza of Title Suit No.176 of 2004 produced all the relevant original documents from his custody, such as: letter of allotment, money receipts showing payment of installments in respect of the suit plot, paper handing over possession of the suit plot to him, the standard lease deed, approved plan dated 29-10-1966 by the then DIT for constructing building on the suit plot, permission letter issued by DIT to undertake construction on the suit plot, DCR, mutation khatian, rent receipts and RS Khatian prepared in his name. He has lastly submitted that the trial Court as well as the High Court Division rejected the plaintiff's claim that he was the Executive Engineer of C&B under the Ministry of Communications considering the extraneous matters than the documentary evidence produced by him and thus fell into an error in dismissing the suit.

24. Mr Khalilur Rahman, learned Advocate, entering caveat on behalf of the respondents in CP Nos.2199 of 2011, 1671 and 1672 of 2011, on the other hand, has supported the impugned judgment passed by the learned Judges of the High Court Division affirming those of the trial Court passed in the respective suit. He has submitted that in view of the conflicting pleadings of the respective party, possession of the suit plot was a very vital and crucial issue to be decided in the suits. The trial Court as well as the High Court Division considering the tenancy agreements entered into by late Engineer Shamsul Kabir Humayun Reza of 157, Bara Moghbazar with the tenants, rent receipts, RS Khatian, bills of the utility services, came to the specific finding that the plaintiffs father, Engineer Shamsul Kabir Humayun Reza of 157, Bara Moghbazar had been in possession of the suit plot right from the time of delivery of possession, pursuant to the letter of allotment and rightly decreed Title Suit No.81 of 2006 and dismissed the other two suits. He has further submitted that the findings of the Courts below that plaintiffs' (Title Suit No.81 of 2006) father, late Engineer Shamsul Kabir Humayun Reza of 157, Bara Moghbazar was the actual allottee of the suit plot and he never entered into any contract with defendant No.1, MA Hashem or defendant No.4, MNH Bulu and that defendant No.2, Shamsul Kabir Humayun Reza of Mirpur was a fake person, are concurrent findings of facts based on proper consideration of the evidence on record adduced by the parties, there is no scope of interference by this Court with such findings and, as such, the question of sending the suit or the appeal back on remand to the Court below does not arise at all and he submits that all the petitions be dismissed.

25. Before considering the propriety of the submissions of the respective learned Advocate appearing in four leave petitions, we consider it necessary to see the findings of the Courts below.

26. As already stated hereinbefore, three suits, namely: Title Suit Nos.51 of 2004, 176 of 2004 and 81 of 2006, were disposed of by separate judgments and decrees by the learned Joint District Judge, First Court, Dhaka, although there was only one set of evidence in each suit. And we propose to proceed serially according to the number of the suit.

27. Title Suit No. 51 of 2004:

The trial Court dismissed the suit. One of the findings on which the trial Court dismissed the suit, was that the suit plot was allotted to Shamsul Kabir Humayun Reza, a BSc Engineer of 157, Bara Moghbazar and not to Shamsul Kabir Humayun Reza of Mirpur and that at the time of allotment, he was the Executive Engineer of C&B, Dhaka and he joined the Department in 1952. In coming to the said finding, the trial Court relied upon exhibit-‘ড’, the letter dated 29-10-1966, issued by the then DIT to Shamsul Kabir Humayun Reza of 157, Bara Moghbazar giving permission to carry on with the proposed construction on the suit plot. The trial Court also relied upon the testimony of PW 2 Shammima Zaman, daughter of Shamsul Kabir Humayun Reza of 157, Bara Moghbazar and PW5-Dr Md Arifur Rahman, her husband who was the principal of National Medical College (they were examined in Title Suit No.81 of 2006 and their depositions were adopted in Title Suit No.51 of 2004) and DW 1 who was examined in Title Suit No.176 of 2004 on behalf of RAJUK. The trial Court came to the further finding that the plaintiff could not file any document to show that Shamsul Kabir Humayun Reza of Mirpur was a BSc Engineer and Executive Engineer in C&B. From the plaint, it was clear that the address of defendant No.1, Shamsul Kabir Humayun Reza was given at House No.31, Lane No.6, Section 6/ A, Mirpur, Dhaka. Plaintiff, MA Hashem, in his deposition as PW 1, admitted that as defendant No 1 was not registering the sale deed in respect of the suit plot, he through his lawyer issued a legal notice to him on 20-12-2003 who duly received the same. The trial Court observed that question could not arise that Shamsul Kabir Humayun Reza, a BSc Engineer, who joined Bengal Development Company as Director after leaving the job of C&B and subsequently, became a Director of Uttara Bank Limited, would construct a house atMirpur and reside there. The trial Court came to the further specific finding that the plaintiff on 29-12-2002 entered into the agreement for purchasing the suit plot with Shamsul Kabir Humayun Reza of Mirpur, who was not the allottee of the suit plot. Plaintiff, MA Hashem, in the written statement filed in Title Suit No.81 of 2006 (he was defendant No.1 in the suit) clearly asserted that Shamsul Kabir Humayun Reza of 157, Bara Moghbazar was the original allottee, but he as a witness deposed that Shamsul Kabir Humayun Reza of Mirpur was the allottee of the suit plot and the Court observed that “তাহা হইলে বাদী এম এ হাশেম সাহেব নিজেই সম্পত্তির মূল বরাদ্ধ গ্রহীতার বিষয়ে দুই রকম বক্তব্য প্রদান করিতেছে”।  The two other witnesses examined by the plaintiff were none, but his employees. The trial Court further noticed that DWI (Abu Bakkar Siddique examined on behalf of RAJUK in Title Suit No.176 of 2004) stated in his evidence that the suit plot was a residential plot and an application was filed to convert the same as an industrial plot. The DW further stated that letter was issued to Shamsul Kabir Humayun Reza on 14-8-2001 to deposit a sum of Taka 38,28,125 as a conversion fee and pursuant to the said letter, he deposited a sum of Taka 38,28,125 on 25-9-2001. Shamsul Kabir Humayun Reza filed an application to RAJUK on 26-9-2001 to accept the said transfer fees. The application for conversion of the suit plot and the letter written by Shamsul Kabir Humayun Reza were proved by DWI as exhibits ‘ণ’ and ‘থ’. The trial Court further found that Shamsul Kabir Humayun Reza gave power of attorney to Eastern Housing Limited to do all acts in respect of the suit plot (in Bangla it has been written নলিশি সম্পত্তির যাবতীয় কার্য পরিচালনার জন্য) and to accept the said power of attorney, he filed an application to RAJUK on 24-12-2003 and RAJUK approved the power of attorney on 5-1-2004 and the said letter was filed and proved and marked as exhibit ‘ন’. There was no paper with RAJUK to show that application was filed to it for permission to sell the suit plot by Shamsul Kabir Humayun Reza of Mirpur. The trial Court concluded that the plaintiff did not enter into the agreement on 29-12-2002 with the original allottee to purchase the suit plot, so his suit for specific performance of contract was not maintainable.

28. Title Suit No.176 of 2004:

The trial Court dismissed the suit on the findings, inter alia, that plaintiff, Shamsul Kabir Humayun Reza, was not the original allottee, therefore, RAJUK could not give permission to him to sell the suit plot and that RAJUK recognized Shamsul Kabir Humayun Reza of 157, Bara Moghbazar as the allottee of the suit plot and also approved Eastern Housing Limited as his attorney; the plaintiff's suit was not maintainable. In coming to the said findings, the trial Court held that the suit plot was allotted to Engineer Shamsul Kabir Humayun Reza, who was the Executive Engineer of C&B and that RS khatian No.1174 in respect of the suit plot was recorded in the name of Shamsul Kabir Humayun Reza of 157, Bara Moghbazar; plaintiff, Shamsul Kabir Humayun Reza as PW 1 in his cross-examination clearly stated that he never resided at 157, Bara Moghbazar and while he deposed, he was residing at 911/1 Shewrapara as a tenant of one Monowara; the suit plot was allotted to Executive Engineer, Shamsul Kabir Humayun Reza and admittedly the plaintiff was Diploma Engineer and therefore, it was not believable that he could join a big Department like C&B as Assistant Engineer in 1950/1952 and was promoted as the Executive Engineer in 1956/57 and therefore, it could not be accepted that the plaintiff got the allotment of the suit plot as Executive Engineer. The trial Court further noticed that PW 1 (Shamsul Kabir Humayun Reza) admitted in his cross- examination that in 1965, he was a voter at Ishwardi and after liberation of Bangladesh, he participated in an election at Fulbaria under the district of Dinajpur, which was not believable, asit was not natural that a person, who served as an Executive Engineer, would go to an Upazila and participate in the election. The trial Court further observed that it was not natural that a person, who became the Executive Engineer, would live at Pabna, then at Dinajpur and then in Dhaka. The trial Court further observed that it was also not believable that a person, who got a plot from RAJUK at Gulshan, would reside at Ishwardi in the district of Pabna and then at Fulbaria in the district of Dinajpur. Although the plaintiff claimed that he accepted domicile certificate but that was not with him. The trial Court further observed that the plaintiff in his cross-examination admitted that though he served as an Executive Engineer in C&B, he did not open any bank account, which also proved that he was not an Executive Engineer, because the salary of a first class employee is given through cheque and in the absence of bank account, he could not draw his salary. The trial Court further noticed that the plaintiff admitted in his cross-examination that he had gone to India in 1958 and married his cousin (খালাতো বোন) at 'Arai' in India. The trial Court noticed that a person, who migrated to this country, in 1947 and was a high official in C&B, could not go to India without the permission of his office or the Government for marrying there. PW2, a guard of Sonali Bank, Motijheel Branch, was disbelieved by the trial Court on the ground that when the plaintiff admitted that he had no bank account, it was not believable that he would be acquainted with a guard of Sonali Bank. The trial Court also observed that it was not natural that the plaintiff, who claimed to be an Executive Engineer, would develop a relationship with a guard of a bank. The trial Court disbelieved PW 3 on the ground that he deposed that he got himself acquainted with the plaintiff through one of the relative, namely, Shamim; when PWI in his cross-examination admitted that he had no relative in this country. (PWs 2 and 3 were examined to prove plaintiff's possession in the suit plot.) The trial Court relied on the evidence of DW1, an official of RAJUK that no application was filed by the plaintiff, Shamsul Kabir Humayun Reza, to RAJUK for permission to sell the suit plot to MNH Bulu and that no application was filed by the plaintiff to RAJUK on 18-2-2004 to sell the suit plot. The trial Court further held that RAJUK did not recognize the plaintiff of the instant suit as the allottee of the suit plot, rather RAJUK admitted Shamsul Kabir Humayun Reza of 157, Bara Moghbazar as the allottee and approved the power of attorney given by him to Eastern Housing Limited.

29. Title Suit No.81 of 2006:

The trial Court decreed the suit on the findings, inter alia, that the plaintiffs proved conclusively that the suit plot was allotted to Shamsul Kabir Humayun Reza of 157, Bara Moghbazar; Shamsul Kabir Humayun Reza of Bara Moghbazar after obtaining permission from RAJUK vide letter dated 29-10-1996, exhibit-16(ka) (the letter was also proved by DW1 in Title Suit No.176 of 2004 as exhibit-V) constructed building on the suit plot by mortgaging the same with the House Building Finance Corporation as apparent from exhibit-'17', the loan sanction letter dated 2-3-1967 issued by the House Building Finance Corporation and had been in possession of the building by letting out the same to various tenants vide exhibit-'18 series. The trial Court further observed that the first tenancy agreement was entered into in 1967 wherein the address of Shamsul Kabir Humayun Reza was written as 157, Bara Moghbazar and in all the subsequent tenancy agreements his address was also written as 157, Bara Moghbazar. The trial Court further found that the plaintiff of Title Suit No.176 of 2004, who was impleaded in the instant suit (Title Suit No.81 of 2006) as defendant No.3, stated in his examination-in-chief as PW1 that he was residing at 911/1 East Sheurapara, Thana Kafrul and in cross-examination, he stated that he never resided at 157, Bara Moghbazar. The trial Court came to further finding that as per own admission, defendant No.3 (plaintiff of Title suit No.176 of 2004) neither resided at Bara Moghbazar nor at the suit plot, but he claimed to have prayed for permission to sell the suit plot. The trial Court further noticed that PW1 (Shamsul Kabir Humayun Reza examined in Title Suit No.176 of 2004) stated in his examination-in-chief that he had no issue and that though he got the domicile certificate, but he would not be able to file the same as the same was not with him. The trial Court concluded by saying that defendant No.3, Shamsul Kabir Humayun Reza was not the owner of the suit plot and that the plaintiffs' father Shamsul Kabir Humayun Reza was the owner thereof and had the right, title and possession therein and after his death, the plaintiffs became its owners. The trial Court further noticed that defendant No.1 in his written statement admitted that the plaintiffs father was the owner of the suit plot and thus admitted the plaint case. The trial Court further noticed that in the agreement for sale between defendant No.1 and Shamsul Kabir Humayun Reza, his address was mentioned at the suit plot and at present Mirpur, whereas the owner of the suit plot was Shamsul Kabir Humayun Reza of 157, Bara Moghbazar. The trial Court on consideration of the evidence of MA Hashem examined in Title Suit No.51 of 2004 further held that Shamsul Kabir Humayun Reza of Mirpur neither being the allottee of the suit plot nor being in possession thereof, the agreement, even if, entered into by him with defendant No.1 was not "Executable". The trial Court also found that DW 1 (examined in Title Suit No.176 of 2004 on behalf of RAJUK) clearly admitted in his deposition that in the Nathi of RAJUK, only the names of the plaintiffs were mutated. The trial Court further held that the plaintiffs are in possession of the suit plot and after the death of their father, Engineer Shamsul Kabir Humayun Reza of 157, Bara Moghbazar, became its owner. The trial Court came to further finding that RS record of the suit plot was recorded in the name of the plaintiffs predecessor, Shamsul Kabir Humayun Reza, wherein his address has been clearly mentioned as 157, Bara Moghbazar which fact also confirmed that he was the allottee of the suit plot and since defendant No.1 entered into a contract with fake Shamsul Kabir Humayun Reza of Mirpur without permission of RAJUK and claimed possession in the suit plot and thus also posed threat to the plaintiffs' possession therein as well as their title, their suit as framed was maintainable and they were entitled to get the relief prayed for in the suit.

30. Now the findings of the High Court Division:

As stated earlier, the appeals were heard and disposed of by a Division Bench. The learned Judges dismissed all the appeals. They gave separate findings and decisions in respect of each of the suits giving rise to the appeals. From the impugned judgment, it further appears that the learned presiding Judge after giving his findings/decisions as to the merits of the respective appeal made some observations comments and remarks about the inhabitants of India, a part of it being Bangladesh, its political leaders and the independence of judiciary (the observations will be quoted hereinafter at an appropriate place). The learned second Judge differed with those observations/comments and remarks of the learned presiding Judge and wrote his own views separately on the issues involved in each of the appeals. Therefore, we cannot help but to refer to the findings of both the learned Judges and in doing so, there may be repetitions.

31. Civil Petition for Leave to Appeal No.1671 of 20 11:

This leave petition has been filed against the judgment and decree passed by the High Court Division in First Appeal No.357 of 2009, which was preferred against the judgment and decree passed in Title Suit No.51 of 2004. The suit was dismissed by the trial Court. The learned presiding Judge formulated two points for decision: (a) whether the plaintiff entered into the agreement "with false person in the name of Shamsul Kabir Humayun Reza" and (b) whether the plaintiff proved the necessary ingredients to get the decree for specific performance of contract. The learned Judge on consideration of the evidence on record and the order sheets of the suit, particularly, from 8-2-2004 to 15-7-2004, came to the specific findings that the plaintiff entered into agreement with Shamsul Kabir Humayun Reza of Mirpur and he filed the suit for specific performance of contract against him. Shamsul Kabir Humayun Reza of Mirpur was not the actual allottee of the suit plot and it was Shamsul Kabir Humayun Reza of 157, Bara Moghbazar who was the actual allottee and he died on 2-2-2004, i.e. six days before filing of the suit (the suit was filed on 8-2-2004). The learned Judge noticed that on 18-3-2004, the plaintiff filed an application for temporary injunction for restraining defendant No.1 (Shamsul Kabir Humayun Reza) and defendant No.2 (Akhtaruzzaman, Director, Eastern Housing Ltd.) from changing the nature and character of the suit plot, in any manner, (although Shamsul Kabir Humayun Reza of 157, Bara Moghbazar died on 2-2-2004) and the Court by order dated 25-3-2004 issued notices upon the defendants to show cause as to why an order of temporary injunction should not be granted. Considering the order sheets from 8-2-2004 up to 15-7-2004, the learned Judge observed that the plaintiff did not take any step in the suit either for substituting or adding the heirs of Shamsul Kabir Humayun Reza of 157, Bara Moghbazar which indicated that the plaintiff entered into the agreement with Shamsul Kabir Humayun Reza of Mirpur and filed the suit against him. And had the plaintiff-MA Hashem, accepted defendant Nos. l(a) to l(c) as the heirs of Shamsul Kabir Humayun Reza of 157, Bara Moghbazar than he would not have moved the High Court Division against the order passed by the learned Additional District Judge, T" Court, Dhaka in Civil Revision No.358 of 2004 adding them as the heirs of late Shamsul Kabir Humayun Reza. The learned Judge further noticed that on an application filed by defendant No.2 (name has been mentioned hereinbefore) for the personal appearance of defendant No.1, Shamsul Kabir Humayun Reza of Mirpur, the learned Joint District Judge by order dated 27-5-2004 directed the plaintiff to produce defendant No.1, but he failed to produce him in Court and till that time (27-5-2004), the plaintiff did not accept Shamsul Kabir Humayun Reza of 157, Bara Moghbazar as his vendor. The learned Judge further observed "As I find his presence in the suit after six months of filing of the suit and thereafter he disappeared and the plaintiff could not or did not satisfy the Court by producing any documents that Shamsul Kabir Humayun Reza, defendant No.1 resides at Bara Moghbazar at any point of time." The plaintiff made prayer for specific performance of contract from defendant No.1 and also for a direction upon him to obtain necessary sale permission from RAJUK in his favour and had he disowned defendant No.1 than after substitution or addition of defendant Nos.1(a)-1(e), the prayer and other incidental statements of the plaint ought to have been amended, but the plaintiff did not amend the plaint which showed that till hearing, he claimed original defendant No.1, i.e. Shamsul Kabir Humayun Reza of Mirpur as his vendor. The learned Judge held that "Thereby it can safely be concluded that so called bainapatra or agreement for sale was entered into by plaintiff with the per- son whom the plaintiff by his own act sometime did and sometime did not recognize as his vendor as plaintiff allowed defendant Nos. 1 (a) to 1(e) to substitute in place of defendant No.1 who did not recognize defendant No.1 as their father as defendant Nos. 1 (a) to 1(e) did not step aside from their original position that they are not substituted heirs of defendant No.1 of Mirpur but they are the heirs of Shamsul Kabir Humayun Reza of 157, Bara Magbazar against whom the suit has not been filed and no agreement or bainapatra was arrived at by plaintiff."

32. The learned Judge further held that from the conduct of the plaintiff, it was clear that he, in order to grab the suit plot at a meager price of Taka 1,65,00,000, sometime accepted Shamsul Kabir Humayun Reza of Mirpur as his vendor and sometime Shamsul Kabir Humayun Reza of Bara Magbazar as his vendor and having dilemma lastly "gulped" Shamsul Kabir Humayun Reza of Sara Moghbazar as his vendor by accepting the heirs added/substituted in place of defendant No.1, but without amending the material statements or prayer of the plaint. The learned Judge further observed that the agreement was entered into on 29-12-2001 in between MA Hashem (the plaintiff) and Shamsul Kabir Humayun Reza of Mirpur and in the plaint, the address of Shamsul Kabir Humayun Reza was given at House No.31, Lane 6, Section 6/1, Mirpur, Dhaka, although from the protest notice published in the daily Janakantha and the corrigendum thereto, it appeared that before filing the suit, the plaintiff could know that the address of Shamsul Kabir Humayun Reza was 157, Bara Moghbazar, Dhaka. The learned Judge considering the agreement, the evidence of the PWs and the letter allegedly written by the plaintiff to defendant No.1, Shamsul Kabir Humayun Reza of Mirpur and the legal notices issued by his lawyer on his behalf (exhibits 8/1, 8/2, 9/1, 10/1,10/2,11/1, 11/2) held that the execution of bainapatra was suspicious and that no money was transacted and when there was doubt as to the payment of earnest money, decree for specific performance of contract could not be passed. In this regard, the learned Judge referred to the evidence of PW1, MA Hashem that he would not produce supporting documents "in the matter of passing of earnest money." The learned Judge further observed that on the question of possession, the plaintiff did not "state a single word in his plaint as to whether he was inducted into possession or not, although exhibit-2 was entered on 29-12-2002." The learned presiding Judge further observed that in the plaint, the plaintiff stated that he went to the suit property on 22-12-2003 and found that some people were taking step for construction upon removal of the old structure, but there was no explanation "as to the fact that old structure belonged to whom". The plaintiff did not also inform the matter to defendant No.1 with whom he allegedly entered into the contract or to RAJUK, the lessor, either any GD entry was made or any legal proceeding was taken about the matter and he kept mum till filing the suit on 8-2-2004, i.e. about 8( eight) months from the said date. The learned Judge further observed that in the bainapatra and also in the deposition of PWI, it has been mentioned that all the original papers and documents, i.e. allotment letter of DIT (exhibit-I), standard lease agreement of DIT (exhibit-2), payment of installment receipts from National Bank (exhibit-3) were handed over to the plaintiff; defendant Nos. 1 (a) to 1(e) produced before the Court 28 original deeds documents of them exhibits 1 to 5 "in original copies produced, proved and exhibited in the suit. Plaintiff after finding that original deeds were produced from the side of defendant Nos. 1(a) to 1(e) did not challenge as to genuinity of them at least, exhibits 1 to 5 of the defendants documents ought to be challenged but he did not." In the concluding paragraph, the learned Judge gave the finding that the "plaintiff's case for specific performance of contract is totally a hopeless case."

33. The learned second Judge, while dismissing the appeal, gave clear finding that it was manifest that Shamsul Kabir Humayun Reza of Mirpur and Engineer Md Shamsul Kabir Humayun Reza of Gulshan was not the same person and that the dispute regarding the title of the suit plot was started before "expiry of the Engineer Md Shamsul Kabir Humayun Reza who died on 2-2-2004", whereas the plaintiff filed the suit on 8-2-2004 impleading defendant No.2, Akhtaruzzaman, a Director of Eastern Housing Limited, as the attorney of late Engineer Shamsul Kabir Humayun Reza showing defendant No.1 as his vendor. The learned second Judge noticed that on 20-3-2004, 3rd party, i.e. the heirs of late Shamsul Kabir Humayun Reza, filed an application in the suit under Order I, rule 10 of the Code of the Civil Procedure for adding them as defendants in the suit as his heirs and not to substitute them in place of defendant No.1 stating that they got the information that the suit was filed by the plaintiff on the basis of false bainapatra created by him with fictitious person having no title and interest and possession in the suit plot, but the learned Joint District Judge by his order dated 15-7-2004 allowed the application for addition of party, not as the third party, but as the heirs of defendant No.1. The learned Judge also considered the fact that on 23-3-2004, the plaintiff by filing a fresh vokalatnama prayed for hearing the application for temporary injunction filed on 18- 3-2004 and the Court on hearing the application issued notices upon defendant Nos.1 and 2 to show cause as to why an order of temporary injunction should not be granted and on 27-5- 2004 defendant Nos.1 and 2 filed hajira and then again concluded that "So, from the record and conduct of the plaintiff it was crystal clear that defendant No.1, Shamsul Kabir Humayun Reza of Mirpur and Engineer Shamsul Kabir Humayun Reza of 157 Bara Maghbazar is not the same and one person." Considering the evidence of PW1 (the plaintiff) the learned Judge came to the findings that "it is further manifest" that the plaintiff at the time of filing the suit and sometime after, did not admit rather denied the existence, right, title and possession of Engineer Md Shamsul Kabir Humayun Reza of Bara Moghbazar, later on only to escape and justify his earlier false activities admitted "with a device that defendant Nos.1(a) to 1(e) are the legal heirs of defendant No.1, i.e. Shamsul Kabir Humayun Reza of Mirpur" by substituting them. The plaintiff neither made a contract for purchasing the suit plot with a genuine person nor the documents submitted in Court were genuine and on the basis of those documents, he could not get decree for specific performance of contract and that the total case of the plaintiff was nothing but a got-up story only to grab the suit plot on the basis of forged deed and papers with the help of a fake person. The plaintiff failed to prove his alleged bainapatra and part payment of consideration money. The learned Judge disbelieved PWs 2 and 3 on the finding that they being employees of the plaintiff could not but oblige their master. The learned Judge also took adverse presumption against the plaintiff for non-examination of a vital and important witness, namely, Shawkat Ali, who figured as a witness in the bainapatra and lived in the same holding with defendant No.1, Shamsul Kabir Humayun Reza at Mirpur. In this regard, the learned Judge further opined that the identity of defendant No.1 could only be clarified by Shawkat Ali, who is also a relative of the plaintiff. The learned Judge concluded that "plaintiff not only failed to prove his own case but produced some forged and fabricated deeds and papers because from the facts and circumstances it (sic) clear that plaintiff PW.1 produced the exhibit 1,2, 3, 4 and 5 in the court showing his vendor as custodian of those exhibit's who failed to appear before the court and from the materials on record it is proved that he is none but a fake person. So, exhibit "6" (the agreement) is nothing but obviously the creation of forgery only to grab the suit property. Furthermore, the defence story and facts as set forth are quite believable not only by' oral evidences, however, facts and circumstances with possession and documentary evidences, as find support from the testimony of PWs of title suit No.81 of 2006 which has been adopted in this case with the consent of all the parties."

34. Civil Petition for Leave Appeal No. 2198 of 2011:

This petition has been filed by the plaintiff, Shamsul Kabir Humayun Reza of Title Suit No.176 of 2004 against the judgment and decree passed in First Appeal No.327 of 2009 which was filed against the judgment and decree passed in the said suit. In this appeal, the learned presiding Judge formulated only one point for determination namely, "whether there was any application pending before the defendants to be considered by RAJUK." It may be stated that in the suit 4(four) persons, namely: the Chairman, RAJUK, (ii) the Member (Estate) (iii) RAJUK, (iv) the Deputy Director (Estate), RAJUK were impleaded as defendants. In the context, it is necessary to state that plaintiff, Shamsul Kabir Humayun Reza, filed the suit for the principal relief as under:

"A. Pass a decree in favour of the plaintiff and against the defendants declaring that the plaintiff is legally entitled to get sale permission and the defendants are also bound to issue sale permission in respect of the scheduled property by receiving the proper transfer fees from the plaintiff as per rule of RAJUK."

Then by way of amendment prayer 'B' was added to the effect:

"B. Pass a decree in favour of the plaintiff and against the defendants declaring that the defendants are bound to dispose of the application dated 18-2-2004 filed by the plaintiff for sale permission in respect of the schedule property within a specified time as directed by the Court by receiving the proper transfer fees as per rule of RAJUK."

35. The learned presiding Judge noticed that on 18-9-2008, i.e. long after 4 years and 4 months of the institution of the suit (the suit was instituted on 26-5-2004) further amendment was made in paragraph 4 of the plaint as to the number of the deed of lease executed and registered by RAJUK, i.e. figure "1198" was replaced by the figure "7198". The learned Judge came to the finding that the plaintiff did not produce any copy of his application filed to RAJUK for sale permission of the suit plot. The learned Judge further noticed that RAJUK in its written statement stated that "such file in the name of Shamsul Kabir Humayun Reza as mentioned is not traceable in the office of RAJUK and I find plaintiff did not produce any copy of his application in the suit or exhibited it." The learned Judge concluded that "So, it may be legally presumed that such application was not filed and alternatively it may be presumed that the suit plot was not allotted to the plaintiff." Considering the deposition of PW1, the learned Judge observed that after his son disappeared with his brother-in-law in 1972 who had allegedly gone to the suit plot in the month of December, 1972, till filing the suit on 26-5-2004, i. e. for the last 32 years, the plaintiff did not go to the suit plot and that he did not also take any step through legal process for recovery of the same and that had he been allotted the suit plot as lessee by RAJUK, he ought to have informed this fact to RAJUK for recovery of possession, but he did nothing. Although, in his deposition, the plaintiff claimed that he had lived in the suit plot till 1971 and then left the suit plot for security reason keeping his Caretaker, Abul Khair and then again came back there, in 1972, he did not examine Abul Khair. The plaintiff did not also produce his domicile certificate. The learned Judge further noticed that the plaintiff stated in his deposition that after coming to Dhaka, he resided at 154, Bara Moghbazar thereafter at Mirpur with his uncle, but the RS Khatian was prepared. in the name of Shamsul Kabir Humayun Reza of 157, Bara Moghbazar and not in the name of Shamsul Kabir Humayun Reza of 154, Bara Moghbazar. The learned Judge further observed that if it was true that after getting permission for construction from RAJUK, the plaintiff constructed a two storied building on the suit plot then a man of prudence could not sit idol and keep mum when he found his property occupied by a third person and dismantling the building standing therein. The learned Judge came to the finding that the claim of possession of the plaintiff in the suit plot was not true. The plaintiff claimed to be a Government employee, but he had no bank account, then how he could draw his salary, because salary could not be paid in hand or in cash but through a bank account. The learned Judge' further noticed that the plaintiff, Shamsul Kabir Humayun Reza first produced "allotment deed as deed No.1198 and no sooner its genuinity when challenged he produce (sic) deed No.7198 shows how quick such forgery could (sic) made. Both the deeds are on record."

36. The learned second Judge considering the evidence of the plaintiff as PW1 came to the finding that though PW 1 (Shamsul Kabir Humayun Reza) claimed that he was an Executive Engineer, his testimony regarding service book, ACR and organogram clearly proved that he was not a service holder at all. In coming to the said finding, the learned Judge observed that "He stated that he was executive engineer but could not understand the ACR and organogram." The learned Judge came to further finding that the documents filed by the plaintiff exhibited in the suit clearly showed that those were prepared in the name of Shamsul Kabir Humayun Reza of 157, Bara Moghbazar, which was admittedly the permanent address of Engineer Shamsul Kabir Humayun Reza, a different person. The learned Judge further held that the suit of the plaintiff for sale permission simplicity to sell the suit plot was not maintainable as he himself admitted in the plaint as well as in his deposition that he was out of possession and that Title Suit No.81 of 2006 filed by the heirs of Shamsul Kabir Humayun Reza of 157, Bara Moghbazar for declaration of title and permanent injunction heard simultaneously by the same Court (the plaintiff was defendant No.3 in the suit) was decreed. The learned Judge rejected the objection raised by Mr Abdul Wadud Bhuiyan who also appeared before the High Court Division that the trial Court erred in law "in adopting the evidence of PW1 as a (sic) evidence of DW1 in the respective cases" as such adoption was done in violation of section 33 of the Evidence Act, on the view that the said section had no manner of application as the plaintiff of both suits, i.e. Title Suit Nos.51 of 2004 and 176 of 2004 failed to prove the respective case. More so, from the record, it appeared that the defendants by filing application on 30-6-2009 prayed for adoption of their depositions given as PW 1 in Title Suit Nos.176 of 2004 and 51 of 2004 respectively as deposition of DW in Title Suit No.81 of 6 at their own risk." The learned Judge further held that the appellants were not entitled to raise objection as to the adoption of evidence before the Appellate Court as no objection was raised either before the trial Court or any such ground was taken in the Memorandum of Appeal and that no one was entitled to get benefit of his own wrong. On the above findings, the learned Judge concluded that the trial Court did not commit any error of law in dismissing the suit.

37. Civil Petition for Leave to Appeal Nos. 2199 of 20 11 and 1672 of 2011:

These two leave petitions have been filed by Shamsul Kabir Humayun Reza and MA Hashem respectively against the judgment and decree passed by the High Court Division in First Appeal Nos.346 and 357 of 2009 which were preferred against the judgment and decree passed in Title Suit No.81 of 2006. First Appeal No.346 of 2009 was preferred by Shamsul Kabir Humayun Reza and First Appeal No.358 of2009 was preferred by MA Hashem, who were defendant Nos.3 and 1, respectively in the suit. Both the appeals have been dismissed.

38. The learned presiding Judge observed that though defendant No.3 (also plaintiff in Title Suit No.176 of 2004) claimed that he was in possession in the suit plot, but "no where he ever stated how he was inducted into possession and when he was dispossessed." There was only a solitary statement in his deposition that in 1970, (it will be 1972) he sent his brother-in-law and the only son to the suit plot and thereafter, both of them disappeared. If that fact was true, then the defendant and other family members would have left no stone unturned to find out the whereabouts of his only son and brother-in-law, but nothing was done. PW 1 stated that he made a GD entry, but the copy of GD entry was not produced. The learned Judge observed that since the time of missing of his only son and the brother-in-law till the date of hearing of the appeal, the defendant did not take any step either to recovery the possession of the suit plot or for a declaration that he was the lawful owner thereof. Considering the documents of the plaintiffs, namely: allotment letter (exhibit-1); general power of attorney executed by the heirs of Shamsul Kabir Humayun Reza of 157, Bara Moghbazar (exhibits-2, 3, and 3Ka) in favour of Eastern Housing Limited; matriculation certificate of Shamsul Kabir Humayun Reza of 157, Bara Moghbazar (exhibit-4); intermediate certificate of Shamsul Kabir Humayun Reza of 157 Bara Moghbazar (exhibit-4Ka); BSc Engineering certificate Part II of Shamsul Kabir Humayun Reza of 157, Bara Moghbazar (exhibit-4Kha); BSc Engineering certificate of Shamsul Kabir Humayun Reza of 157, Bara Moghbazar (exhibit- 4Ga); Pakistani domicile declaration of Shamsul Kabir Humayun Reza (exhibit-5); deed of sale executed in favour of AZM Sanaullah dated 24-9- 1957 (exhibit-6); deed of sale executed by AZM Sanaullah dated 24-9-1957 in favour of Shamsul Kabir Humayun Reza in respect of the property at Bara Moghbazar; deed of heba dated 17-8-1979 executed by Shamsul Kabir Humayun Reza in favour of his wife, Selima Begum (exhibit-7); approved plan of DIT (exhibit-8); voter identity card of Shamsul Kabir Humayun Reza (exhibit- 9); passport of Shamsul Kabir Humayun Reza (exhibit-'9Ka); certificate issued by the Government of Canada in favour of Shamsul Kabir Humayun Reza (exhibit-10); promotion letters (exhibit-11 series); allotment letter of DIT in favour of Shamsul Kabir Humayun Reza (exhibit-12); receipts showing payment of installments (exhibits-12Ka to 12Gha); the letters written by Shamsul Kabir Humayun Reza of 157, Bara Moghbazar to DIT informing about the fact of payment of installments (exhibits 13 uma to 13Cha); letter written by the then DIT to Shamsul Kabir Humayun Reza as to the delivery of possession of the suit plot and also asking him to deposit Taka 51 (exhibit-14); Standard lease deed of the suit plot (exhibit-15); letter of DIT approving plan (exhibit-16); photostat copy of approved plan of DIT (exhibit-16Ka); letter of House Building Finance Corporation sanctioning loan (exhibit-17); mortgaged deed executed by Shamsul Kabir Humayun Reza of 157, Bara Moghbazar in favour of House Building Finance Corporation (exhibit-17Ka); tenancy agreements between Shamsul Kabir Humayun Reza and the tenants spreading over the period from 1967 to 2000 (exhibit-18 series); letters of WASA for water connection and bills showing payment of gas, electricity and WASA charges (exhibits-19 series); municipal assessment tax for the period 1967 to 1968 (exhibit-20); the receipts of tax as to the payment of tax of the Municipality for the period 2003 to 2004 (exhibit-20Kha); receipt showing payment ofland tax (exhibits-21-21 Na); RS Khatian No. 1174 recorded in the name of Shamsul Kabir Humayun Reza of 157, Bara Moghbazar (exhibit-22); Memorandum and Articles of Association of Bengal Development Corporation Limited (exhibits-23-23Ka); audit reports of Uttara Bank Limited for the period 1996-2001 (exhibits-24-24Uma); letter of DIT to Shamsul Kabir Humayun Reza of 157, Bara Moghbazar regarding fees for changing the suit plot from residential to commercial (exhibit-25); letter issued by RAJUK to Shamsul Kabir Humayun Reza of 157, Bara Moghbazar approving the conversion of the suit plot into a commercial plot from residential plot (exhibit-26); certified copy of the general power of attorney executed by Shamsul Kabir Humayun Reza of 157, Bara Moghbazar in favour of Eastern Housing Limited (exhibit-27); approval letter of RAJUK in respect of the power of attorney given by Shamsul Kabir Humayun Reza to Eastern Housing Limited (exhibit-28); approved plan (exhibits 29-29Kha); notices published in the Daily Janakatha (exhibits-30-30Kha); corrigendum published in the Daily Janakatha (exhibits 31-31Ka); Death certificate of Shamsul Kabir Humayun Reza of 157, Bara Moghbazar (exhibit 32); news published in different news papers by Uttara Bank Limited on the death of Shamsul Kabir Humayun Reza (exhibit-33); Succession certificate issued by the ward Commissioner of Ward No.53, Dhaka City Corporation (exhibit-33Ka); Succession certificate issued by the 3'd Joint District Judge, Dhaka (exhibit-34); plaint of Title Suit No.51 of 2004 (exhibit-35); plaint of Title Suit No.176 of 2004(exhibit-36); pay bills (exhibits-37-37Ka); Passport No.B-57985 of Shamsul Kabir Humayun Reza of 157, Bara Moghbazar (exhibit-38); came to the positive finding that the plaintiffs proved that from 1967 till the hearing of the suit, i.e. for more than 35 years, they had been in possession of the suit plot and such continuous possession gave them title and that the lessor, RAJUK always recognized the plaintiffs as the lessees and it did not challenge their title in the suit plot in any form. The learned Judge further held that the case of defendant No.3, Shamsul Kabir Humayun Reza, could be considered if he were found to be in possession "but his case on possession is down right false" and by his own statement, it was proved that at no point of time, he was in possession thereof. Considering the documents filed by defendant No.3, namely: letter of allotment dated 21-3-1962, receipts showing deposit of installments against the suit plot, letter as to the delivery of possession of the suit plot, approved plan by RAJUK for construction on the suit plot and the certified copy of the RS Khatian (these documents were proved and marked as exhibits 1,2 series, 3, 6 and 9 respectively) further held that "the exhibits although resemble or appear to be true are not genuine or true, but he has manage(sic) to get those document which apparently original documents" to claim title to the suit plot. If the case of defendant No.3 that he was thrown out of his possession of the suit plot after he was inducted therein and that too when his only son with his brother-in-law disappeared in 1972, was true, he would have left no stone unturned to get back his son and brother-in-law and also the possession of the suit plot, but he only made a GD entry and moved the Parliamentary Standing Committee which had no business in the matter "of declaring title and getting possession of a property which can only be done by Court." Another aspect is that defendant No.3 gave his address "in his suit as of the disputed allotted suit plot which shows that he is a fake person as addressless person can be used by any person for any illegal purpose." The learned Judge further observed that it was the case of defendant No.3 that he entered into an agreement with MNH Bulu to sell the suit plot and to prove that fact defendant No.3 could examine Bhulu but he did not which shows that he lives in false in making out his case." The learned Judge found the documents of the plaintiffs genuine considering two vital and relevant facts (i) when on physical verification of the suit plot, a fraction chhatak/decimal was found to be in excess of the allotted plot, the then DIT directed their father (wrongly written as plaintiff) to pay for the excess land and by the 5th deposit excess amount of Taka 51 was paid and (ii) plaintiffs' predecessor, Shamsul Kabir Humayun Reza of Bara Moghbazar took loan from House Building Finance Corporation by mortgaging the suit plot as evidenced by exhibit-17. Defendant No.3 did not produce any paper to show that he was present when the suit property/plot was measured and in fact, he was never inducted into possession of the suit plot.

39. The learned second Judge considered the evidence on record, both oral and documentary, particularly, the documentary evidence (as have been noted hereinbefore while noting the findings of the learned presiding Judge) and came to the specific finding that the plaintiffs' predecessor, Shamsul Kabir Humayun Reza, was the lessee of RAJUK and he had been exercising his right, title and interest over the suit plot from 1962 till his death within the knowledge of all others. The learned Judge came to the further finding that exhibits-15 (lease deed in original), 15(ka) and 15(kha) (signatures of the attesting witnesses) revealed that those were compared with the original standard lease deed kept in the custody of RAJUK and RAJUK recognized Engineer Shamsul Kabir Humayun Reza, as its lessee. Exhibit-14 dated 7-4-1962 showed that possession of the suit plot was handed over to the father of the plaintiffs. The learned Judge separately considered exhibit-12 series, which are the original allotment letter and original copies of receipts of the payments of installements by the plaintiff to the DIT against the suit plot; exhibit-17 , the loan sanction letter by Housing Building Finance Corporation and exhibiit-17Ka dated 17-5-1967, the mortgage deed by which the suit plot was mortgaged by the plaintiffs' father with the House Building Finance Corporation in order to obtain loan; exhibits-18, 19, and 20 series which are the tenancy agreement, the gas bill, electricity bill and City Corporation tax receipts and rent receipts respectively and came to further finding that it was proved "beyond any doubt" that the plaintiffs after getting lease of the suit plot from RAJUK have been in continuous and uninterrupted possession therein. The learned second Judge further considered exhibits-25 and 26 which proved that "earlier" the suit plot was residential plot and at the instance of the plaintiffs' father, it was converted into commercial plot and exhibits-27 and 28 which proved that after conversion, RAJUK accepted the legal transfer fees and exhibit-29, the approved plan of multistoried commercial building by Eastern Housing Limited as the attorney of the plaintiffs father and these showed that RAJUK accepted plaintiffs father, Shamsul Kabir Humayun Reza as its lessee and did not accept Shamsul Kabir Humayun Reza of Mirpur and the living Shamsul Kabir Humayun Reza (defendant No.3). The learned Judge came to further finding that other two Shamsul Kabir Humayun Reza who claimed the suit plot admittedly were not in possession thereof and both of them made agreement for sale of the suit plot "not as admitted lessee of Rajuk because their papers and documents filed in Title Suit No.51 of 2004 and 176 of 2004 never produced before RAJUK' and claimed that they are the allottee of RAJUK."

40. Now, we shall consider the propriety of the submissions made by the learned Advocates of the respective party keeping in view the findings given by the Courts below as summarized herein-before, the pleadings of the parties and the evidence adduced by them and the other materials on record. And in doing so, there may be repetitions as to the findings of the Courts below, but we cannot avoid that in view of the peculiar nature of the suits and the issues involved therein.

41. In these four leave petitions, in fact, three suits are involved being Title Suit Nos.51 of 2004, 176 of 2004 and 81 of 2006. Before considering the propriety of the common submissions, we deem it proper to see the individual merits of the three suits. We shall do that serially.

42. The first suit which comes for consideration is Title Suit No.51 of 2004. In this suit, MA Hashem was the plaintiff and the suit was filed for specific performance of contract. The trial Court as well as the High Court Division dismissed the suit. It does not require any elaboration as regards the burden of the plaintiff to prove his case, because, by now, it is well settled that the plaintiff has to succeed on his own legs and he cannot succeed on the weakness of the defendant's case, if there be any. It is also well settled that even a suit is heard ex-parte, the plaintiff has to prove his case.

43. In a suit for specific performance of contract, the plaintiff has to prove that there was a valid contract entered into by him with the owner of the suit plot/the suit land, the consideration passed as a part performance of the contract and that the plaintiff was willing to perform his rest part as per the terms of the contract. Let us see how far the plaintiff proved the above things.

44. In the suit (Title Suit No.51 of 2004), the plaintiff impleaded one Shamsul Kabir Humayun Reza as defendant No.1 showing his address at House No.31 , Lane No.6, Section 6/ A, Mirpur, Dhaka as his vendor and prayed for specific performance of contract against him. The other defendants, in the suit, were: Md Akhtaruzzaman, Director, Eastern Housing Limited, Chairman RAJUK and the People's Republic of Bangladesh represented by the Deputy Commissioner, Dhaka. Prayer was also made for a direction upon defendant No.1 to obtain necessary sale permission from RAJUK (defendant No.3) and for a further direction upon RAJUK to accord necessary permission for sale of the suit plot to the plaintiff. In the suit, the heirs (sons and daughters) of Engineer Shamsul Kabir Humayun Reza of 157, Bara Moghbazar filed an application for adding them as defendants asserting categorically that their father, late Engineer Shamsul Kabir Humayun Reza, was the owner of the suit plot who died on 2-2-2004. After the filing of the said application, plaintiff MA Hashem filed an application for substituting the heirs of Shamsul Kabir Humayun Reza bf 157, Bara Moghbazar as the heirs of defendant No.1, Shamsul Kabir Humayun Reza of Mirpur, The learned Joint District Judge rejected the application filed by the sons and the daughters of Shamsul Kabir Humayun Reza of 157, Bara Moghbazar, but allowed the prayer of substitution made by the plaintiff. This substitution was not accepted by the heirs of Shamsul Kabir Humayun Reza of 157, Bara Moghbazar and they filed a civil revision being No.358 of 2004 before the District Judge, Dhaka which was allowed by the learned Additional District Judge, 7111 Court, Dhaka and thus, they were added in the suit as the heirs of late Shamsul Kabir Humayun Reza of 157, Bara Moghbazar and not as the heirs of defendant No.1, that is, Shamsul Kabir Humayun Reza of Mirpur, but challenging this order passed in the civil revision, plaintiff MA Hashem filed Civil Revision No.4608 of 2004 before the High Court Division and eventually, the Rule was made absolute and the order passed by the learned Additional District Judge, was set-aside and thus, the .heirs of Shamsul Kabir Humayun Reza of 157, Bara Moghbazar remained on record in Title Suit No.51 of 2004 as the substituted heirs of defendant No.1. However, the heirs of Shamsul Kabir Humayun Reza as defendant Nos. 1(a)-1(e) filed written statement categorically stating that their father, Shamsul Kabir Humayun Reza of 157, Bara Moghbazar was the allottee of the suit plot and he never entered into any contract with MA Hashem to sell the same and Shamsul Kabir Humayun Reza of Mirpur against whom the plaintiff filed the suit for specific performance of contract was a fake person. Therefore, burden was heavily upon plaintiff MA Hashem either to prove that Shamsul Kabir Humayun Reza of Mirpur was the actual allottee of the suit plot, who entered into the contract with him on 29-12-2002 to sell the same and as a part performance of the contract, advance money as claimed by him was paid to him or that Shamsul Kabir Humayun Reza of 157, Bara Moghbazar, the father of defendant Nos.1(a) to 1(e), the actual allottee of the suit plot entered into the contract with him on 29-12-2002 to sell the suit plot and as part performance of the contract, advance payment was made to him. In this regard, it is to be noted that Shamsul Kabir Humayun Reza, plaintiff of Title Suit No.176 of 2004, was not a party in this suit. Interesting thing is that though, in the plaint (of Title Suit No.51 of 1984), MA Hashim claimed that it is defendant No.1, i.e. Shamsul Kabir Humayun Reza of Mirpur, who entered into the contract with him on 29-12-2002 to sell the suit plot to him, in the written statement filed by him in Title Suit No.81 of 2006, (the suit filed by the heirs of Engineer Shamsul Kabir Humayun Reza) clearly admitted that Shamsul Kabir Humayun Reza of 157, Bara Moghbazar was the actual allottee of the suit plot and the plaintiff of Title Suit No.8 of 2006 being his heirs were bound by the terms of the bainapatra executed by their father and thus, plaintiff MA Hashem gave up his plaint case that Shamsul Kabir Humayun Reza of Mirpur was the allottee of the suit plot and he entered into the contract with him on 29- 12-2002 to sell the suit plot. But the plaintiff did not make any corresponding amendment to the plaint, both in the body as well as in the prayer. From the judgment of the trial Court, it appears that it framed 5(five) issues, viz:

“১। বাদীর মোকদ্দমাটি বর্তমান আকারে ও প্রকারে চলিতে পারে কি?

২। বাদীর মোকদ্দমাটি তামাদিতে বারিত কি?

৩। ২৯-১২-২০০২ ইং তারিকের বায়নাটি মূল বারাদ্দ গ্রহীতা সম্পাতন করিয়া দিয়াছিল কি এবং ১ নং বিবাদী রাজউক হইতে বিক্রয়ের অনুমতি প্রাপ্ত হইয়া বাদীকে নালিশী সম্পত্তির দলিল রেজিষ্ট্রি করিয়া দিতে আইনতঃ বাধ্য কি?

৪। ২৯-১২-২০০২ ইং তারিখের বায়নাভূক্ত সম্পত্তির মূল বরাদ্দ গ্রহীতা ১৫৭ নং বড় মগবাজার নিবাদী শামসুল কবির রেজা না মিরপুর নিবাসী শামসুল কবির হুমায়ুন রেজা?

৫। বাদী অত্র মোকদ্দমায় প্রার্থীত মতে ডিক্রী পাওয়ার অধিকারী কি?

45. Of the five issues, issue Nos.3 and 4 clearly show that one of the crucial fact to be decided in the suit was whether the deed of agreement dated 29-12-2002 was executed by the actual allottee of the suit plot, namely: Shamsul Kabir Humayun Reza of 157, Bara Moghbazar or Shamsul Kabir Humayun Reza of Mirpur. The other fact to be decided was whether defendant No.1 (Shamsul Kabir Humayun Reza of Mirpur) was bound to register the kabala in respect of the suit plot on obtaining permission from RAJUK. In order to prove the case, the plaintiff examined three witnesses including himself as PW 1. PW s 2 and 3 were none but his employees. The trial Court discussing the evidence on record came to the specific findings, inter alia, that it is Shamsul Kabir Humayun Reza, a BSc Engineer of 157, Bara Moghbazar, who was allotted the suit plot and not Shamsul Kabir Humayun Reza of Mirpur; at the time of allotment, Shamsul Kabir Humayun Reza of 157, Bara Moghbazar was the Executive Engineer of C&B, Dhaka; from the plaint, it was clear that the address of defendant No.1, Shamsul Kabir Humayun Reza, was given at House No.31, Lane No.6, section 6/1, Mirpur, Dhaka; plaintiff MA Hashem, in his deposition as PWl, admitted that as defendant No.1 was not registering the sale deed in respect of the suit plot, he through his lawyer issued a legal notice on 20-12-2003 to him, who duly received the same; though plaintiff MA Hashem in the written statement filed in Title Suit No.81 of 2006 (MA Hashem was defendant No.1 in the suit) categorically asserted that Shamsul Kabir Humayun Reza of 157, Bara Moghbazar was the original allottee, he, as a witness, deposed that Shamsul Kabir Humayun Reza of Mirpur was the allottee of the suit plot and thus, the plaintiff gave two different versions as to the original allottee of the suit plot; the plaintiff made the agreement on 29-12-2002 with Shamsul Kabir Humayun Reza of Mirpur for purchasing the suit plot, who was not the allottee of the suit plot; as the plaintiff did not enter into the agreement on 29-12-2002 with the original allottee to purchase the suit plot, his suit for specific performance of contract was not maintainable. The trial Court came to those findings, on detailed discussions of the evidence on record, both oral and documentary. The High Court Division as the last Court of fact discussed the evidence on record, both oral and documentary, in a more detailed manner and affirmed the said finding of the trial Court. The High Court Division came to the further findings that the plaintiff made prayer for specific performance of contract from defendant No.1 and also for a direction upon him to obtain necessary sale permission from RAJUK in his favour and had he disowned defendant No.1 than after substitution or addition of defendant Nos. 1(a) to 1(e), the prayer and other incidental statements of the plaint ought to have been amended, but the plaintiff did not amend the plaint which shows that till hearing, he claimed original defendant No.1, i.e. Shamsul Kabir Humayun Reza of Mirpur as his vendor. The learned Judges came to further finding that execution of bainapatra was suspicious and no money was transacted and when there was doubt as to the payment of earnest money, decree for specific performance of contract could not be passed. The learned Judges also came to the finding that Shamsul Kabir Humayun Reza of Mjrpur and Engineer Md Shamsul Kabir Humayun Reza of Gulshan were not the same persons (emphasis supplied); Engineer Shamsul Kabir Humayun Reza died on 2-2-2004, whereas the plaintiff filed the suit on 8-2-2004 impleading Md Shamsul Kabir Humayun Reza as defendant No.1 as his vendor and Md Akhtaruzzaman, a Director of Eastern Housing Limited, the attorney of Engineer late Shamsul Kabir Humayun Reza as defendant No.2; at the time of filing of the suit and also for sometime thereafter, the plaintiff did not admit rather denied the existence, right, title and possession of Engineer Shamsul Kabir Humayun Reza of 157, Bara Moghbazar in the suit plot and later on, only to escape and justify his earlier false activities admitted "with a device that defendant Nos. 1 (a) to 1 (e) are the legal heirs of defendant No.1. i.e. Shamsul Kabir Humayun Reja of Mirpur"  and substituted the heirs of Shamsul Kabir Humayun Reza of Mirpur; the plaintiff neither made a contract for purchasing the suit plot with a genuine person nor the document submitted in Court were genuine and On the basis of those documents he could not get decree for specific performance of contract and that the total case of the plaintiff was nothing put got up story only to grab the suit plot on the basis of forged deed and paper with the help of a fake person. The learned Judges also opined that the identity of defendant No.1 could only be clarified by Showkat Ali who figured as a witness in the agreement and is also a relative of the plaintiff and used to live in the same holding with defendant No.1, Shamsul Kabir Humayun Reza of Mirpur, but he was not examined. The learned Judges gave further finding that the plaintiff not only failed to prove his own case but produced some forged and fabricated deeds and papers and that from the fact; and circumstances it was dear that the plaintiff produced exhibit:;-1. 2. 3. 4 and 5 in the Court "showing his vendor as custodian of those exhibit's who failed to appear before the Court and from the material on record it is proved that he is none but a fake person" and that exhibit-6 (the agreement) was nothing but the creation of forgery only to grab the suit plot. These are all findings of facts.

46. Mr Fida M Kamal, who appeared for the plaintiff leave petitioner, failed to show with reference to the evidence on record that the findings of the Courts below as jotted down hereinabove were either the result of misreading of the evidence on record or non-consideration of the material evidence on record by the Courts below or otherwise perverse.

47. We have also gone through the evidence on record, both oral and documentary. The findings of the Courts below appeared to us based on correct appreciation of the evidence.

48. In view of the findings given by the Courts below, can a suit for specific performance of contract be decreed? A man of even ordinary prudence would answer in the negative. As for ourselves, we do not find any error committed by the High Court Division in concurring with the decision of the trial Court in dismissing the suit factually and legally.

49. Title Suit No.l76 of 2004, giving rise to Civil Petition for Leave to Appeal No.2198 of 2011, was filed by one Shamsul Kabir Humayun Reza impleading the officials of RAJUK only for two reliefs (i) that he was legally entitled to get the sale permission of the suit plot and the defendants were bound to issue such sale permission on receiving the proper transfer fees from him and (ii) that the defendants were bound to dispose of the application filed by him on 18-2-2004 for sale permission of the suit plot within a specified time as to be directed by the Court claiming himself to be the allottee of the suit plot. He claimed that he was an Executive Engineer of C&B under the Ministry of Communications. But in support of his said claim, he did not file a single paper. The trial Court considering the evidence on record, both oral and documentary, dismissed the suit on the clear findings, inter alia, that the suit plot was allotted to Engineer Shamsul Kabir Humayun Reza who was the Executive Engineer of C&B ; the plaintiff was a Diploma Engineer and it was not believable that he could join a big department like C&B as an Assistant Engineer in 1950/1952 and then promoted as Executive Engineer in 1956/57 The trial Court further observed that the plaintiff in his cross-examination admitted that though he served as an Executive Engineer in C&B, he did not open any  bank account, which also proved that he was not an Executive Engineer, because the salary of a first class employee is given through cheque and in the  absence of bank account* he could not draw his  salary and that he was not the allottee of the suit allot. Therefore, RAJUK could not give permission to him to sell the suit plot. RAJUK recognized Shamsul Kabir Humayun Reza of 157, Bara Moghbazar as the allottee of the suit plot and also approved Eastern Housing Limited as his attorney and that the suit was not maintainable. The trial Court further held that the suit plot was allotted to Engineer Shamsul Kabir Humayun Reza, who was an Executive Engineer of C&B and RAJUK did not recognize the plaintiff as the allottee of the suit plot. RS Khatian  No.11174 in respect of the suit plot was recorded  in the name of Shamsul Kabir Humayun Reza of 157, Bara Moghbazar and plaintiff Shamsul Kabir Humayun Reza as PW1 in his cross-examination  clearly stated that he never resided at 157, Bara  Moghbazar and while he deposed, he was residing at 911/1 Shewrapara as a tenant of one Monowara. The trial Court further held that no application was filed to RAJUK by the plaintiff on 18-2-2004 for permission to sell the suit plot to MNH Bulu. While dismissing the first appeal, the learned Judges affirmed the said findings of the trial Court, but giving their own reasonings. The learned Judges gave further finding that the plaintiff did not produce any copy of his application filed to RAJUK for sale permission of the suit plot. The learned Judges taking into notice of the fact that RAJUK in its written statement stated that "such file in the name of Shamsul Kabir Humayun Reza as mentioned is not traceable in the office of RAJUK" concluded that "so, it may be legally presumed that such application was notified and alternatively it may be presumed that the suit plot was not allotted to the plaintiff." The learned Judges came to further finding that documents filed by the plaintiff exhibited in the suit clearly show that those were prepared in the name  of Shamsul Kabir Humayun Reza of 157, Bara Moghbazar which was admittedly the permanent address of Engineer Shamsul Kabir Humayun  Reza, a different person. The learned Judges further held that the suit of the plaintiff for sale permission simpliciter was not maintainable as he himself admitted in the plaint as well as in his deposition that he was out of possession and that Title Suit No.81 of 2006 filed by the heirs of Shamsul Kabir Humayun Reza of 157, Bara Moghbazar for declaration of title and permanent injunction heard simultaneously by the same Court (the plaintiff was defendant No.3 in the suit) was decreed. The learned Judges came to further finding that though PW I (Shamsul Kabir Humayun Reza) claimed that he was an Executive  Engineer, but his testimony regarding Service  Book, ACR, and Organogram clearly proved that he was not a service holder at all. The findings given by the trial Court as well as the High Court Division as to the actual allottee of the suit plot are so specific and well reasoned that we see no scope of touching with those findings. More interesting thing is that the whole case of the plaintiff was that he filed an application to RAJUK on 18-2-2004 to give him permission to sell the suit plot to MNH Bulu, but that application was not disposed of by RAJUK, which gave him the cause of action to file the suit. And, in fact, the plaintiff prayed for a relief to give direction to RAJUK to dispose of his said application within certain specific time fixed by the Court, but no such application was filed before the Court. Thus, the very foundation of the plaintiff's case had no existence. So, the plaintiff was not entitled to get any relief in the suit. Mr Abdul Wadud Bhuiyan and Mr AF Hasan Ariff, appearing for the petitioner, could not lay their hands on any of the material evidence on record to assail the findings jotted down hereinabove either on the ground of misreading or non-consideration of any material evidence by the Courts below and thus, term those as perverse. (The reasonings, given by the trial Court and the learned Judges of the High Court Division in coming to their findings have been exhaustively noted while summarizing their findings, so those are not repeated.)

50. However, Mr Abdul Wadud Bhuiyan has raised a specific objection that in deciding the merits of Title Suit No.176 of 2004, the High Court Division did not consider the evidence of plaintiff, Shamsul Kabir Humayun Reza as PW 1 (deposition of PW1 was also adopted in Title Suit No.81 of 2006 as defendant No.3) that he applied to the Parliamentary Standing Committee to get possession of the suit plot and the Standing Committee heard him and saw his papers, but none appeared on behalf of the defendants (the defendants were RAJUK and its officials). Then the Parliamentary Standing Committee gave letter to RAJUK, who was the actual Humayun Kabir Reza (in Bangla it has been written কে প্রকৃত হুমায়ুন রেজা ছিল)? and on being asked by the Parliamentary Standing Committee, a report was sent to RAJUK from where it appeared that the plaintiff was the Executive Engineer, Design Division (nothing mentioned of which Department or Directorate) on the ground that the Parliamentary Standing Committee had no business to declare title and give possession of a property without considering that there was no legal bar in approaching the Parliamentary Standing Committee for the purpose and the report given to the Committee was an official one. The comments of the High Court Division as objected to by Mr Bhuiyan cannot be considered in isolation. From the judgment, it appears that the High Court Division made the comments after giving clear finding that though the only son and the brother-in-law of plaintiff, Shamsul Kabir Humayun Reza, did not come back after they had allegedly gone to the suit property in December, 1972; till hearing of the appeal, he neither filed any criminal case nor took any step to recover the possession of the suit plot, nor informed the matter to RAJUK nor took any step for declaration of his title to the suit plot for the last 32 years. The High Court Division also took note of the fact that the plaintiff as PW 1 stated in his examination-in-chief that after the death of his son and brother-in-law, he did not even go to the suit plot. The High Court Division further observed that the application filed before the Parliamentary Standing Committee was neither proved nor filed and the file "which according to him was opened was not called for and therefore, disbelieved plaintiff's (Shamsul Kabir Humayun Reza) story of approaching the Parliamentary Standing Committee and then made the comment. To us it also appears unusual and against the common course of natural events and human conduct that an actual allottee or actual owner of a plot at a posh area like Gulshan in Dhaka City shall not go to his owned land for 32 years and moreso. when a building was allegedly constructed thereon(emphasis supplied). We have gone through the evidence of PWl, Shamsul Kabir Humayun Reza. From his evidence, it appears that after examining him partly in Court on 13-3-2008 and 15-4-2008, he got him examined on commission. While examined on commission, on 8-6-2008, by the learned Advocate Commissioner, Jamaluddin Ahmed, he filed the photostat copy of the so called report of the Parliamentary Standing Committee along with other relevant papers (in Bangla it has been written (সংসদীয় কমিটির প্রতিবেদনসহ আনুষাঙ্গিক দলিলাদির) (17 pages), but this was not accepted as an exhibit and rightly so, as the same was not admissible in evidence in the absence of any proof that the same was made from the original. It is to be further noted that no one was examined to prove the report allegedly given by the Parliamentary Standing Committee. Though PW 1 in his examination-in-chief on 15-4-2008 stated that as per the order of the Parliamentary Standing Committee the report came, which was kept with RAJUK. But the report given by the Parliamentary Standing Committee was not called for from RAJUK; onus was squarely upon the plaintiff to prove the report by examining a competent witness as per the rules of evidence. In this connection, it is also very pertinent to state that plaintiff, Shamsul Kabir Humayun Reza though claimed that he joined the then C&B Department under the Ministry of Communications as an Assistant Engineer in 1952 and then was promoted as an Executive Engineer in 1960, in support of his said case, did not file any scrap of paper. Whereas, the plaintiffs of Title Suit No.81 of 2006 in support of their case that their father, late Engineer Shamsul Kabir Humayun Reza, was a BSc Engineer and was the Executive Engineer of C&B and went to Canada for higher training, filed all the relevant papers, such as, BSc Engineering certificate (exhibit-4Gha), certificate issued by the Government of Canada in favour of Shamsul Kabir Humayun Reza (exhibit-10), promotion letters (exhibit-11 series). So, we find no substance in the objection taken by Mr Abdul Wadud Bhuiyan.

51. Title Suit No.81 of 2006 was filed by the sons and the daughters of late Engineer Shamsul Kabir Humayun Reza of 157, Bara Moghbazar. In the suit, the plaintiffs prayed reliefs, amongst others, for declaration of title to the suit plot and for further declarations that defendant No.2, Shamsul Kabir Humayun Reza of House No.31, Lane No.2, Section 6/A, Mirpur, Dhaka, who executed bainapatra on 29-12-2002 as claimed by defendant No.1 and defendant No.3, (plaintiff of Title Suit No.176 of 2004) were not their father and that both the living Shamsul Kabir Humayun Reza were the creations of defendant Nos.1 and 4 (defendant No.4 was MNH Bulu) and that the bainapatra dated 29-12-2002 showing defendant No.2 as its executor as claimed by defendant No.1 and the bainapatra dated 18-1-2004 executed by defendant No.3 in favour of defendant No.4 as claimed by him (defendant No.3) were fraudulent, collusive, illegal, without any consideration and in-executable (অকার্যকর) and were not binding upon them.

52. In the suit, plaintiff of Title Suit No.51 of 2004, MA Hashem and Shamsul Kabir Humayun Reza, plaintiff of Title Suit No.176 of 2004, were impleaded as defendant Nos.1and 3 respectively and Shamsul Kabir Humayun Reza of Mirpur was impleaded as defendant No.2. The trial Court decreed the suit on the clear findings that the plaintiffs proved conclusively that the suit plot was allotted to Shamsul Kabir Humayun Reza of 157 Bara Moghbazar their father and he constructed building therein on obtaining loan by mortgaging the same with House Building Finance Corporation and had been in possession thereof by letting out the same to various tenants as apparent vide exhibit-18 series. Defendant No.3. Shamsul Kabir Humayun Reza was not the owner of the suit plot. The trial Court on consideration of the evidence of MA Hashem examined in Title Suit NO.51 of2004 further held that Shamsul Kabir Humayun Reza of Mirpur neither was the allottee of the suit plot nor was in possession thereof. The agreement, even if, entered into by him with defendant No.1 was not executable. After the death of plaintiffs' father. Engineer Shamsul Kabir Humayun Reza of 157. Bara Moghbazar they became the owners of the suit plot and they were in possession thereof Defendant No.1 entered into a contract with fake Shamsul Kabir Humayun Reza of Mirpur without permission of RAJUK and claimed possession in the suit plot and thus posed threat to the plaintiffs' possession therein as well as their title. So the suit was maintainable. Defendant No.1 in his written statement admitted that the plaintiffs' father was the owner of the suit plot and thus, admitted the plaint case. The trial Court came to the further findings that the RS record of the suit plot was recorded in the name of the plaintiffs' predecessor. Shamsul Kabir Humayun Reza wherein his address had beenc cearly mentioned as 157. Bara Moghbazar which fact also confirmed that he was the allottee of the suit plot. The High Court Division, being the last Court of fact besides affirming the above findings of the trial Court, came to the finding that from 1967 till the hearing of the suit, i.e. more than 35 years, the plaintiffs had been in possession of the suit plot continuously and uninterruptedly and such possession gave them title and RAJUK, lessor always recognized the plaintiffs as lessees. In coming to the said finding, the High Court Division considered the documents filed by the plaintiffs, being 38 in number and the oral evidence as well. The High Court Division found the documents of the plaintiffs genuine. The High Court Division further held that defendant No.3 (plaintiff of Title Suit No.176 of 2004) claimed that he was in possession in the suit plot, but "no where he ever stated how he was inducted into possession and when he was dispossessed." The High Court Division further observed that had it been true that defendant No.3 sent his brother-in-law and the only son to the suit plot in 1972 and from there. both of them disappeared. then the defendant and the other family members would have left no stone untumed to find out their whereabouts but nothing was done. Although PWl Shamsul Kabir Humayun Reza (examined in Title Suit No.176 of 2004) stated that he made a GD entry over the incident. but copy of the GD entry was not produced. The High Court Division further observed that since the time of missing of the only son and the brother-in- law of plaintiff, Shamsul Kabir Humayun Reza. till the hearing of the appeals. he did not take any step either to recover the possession of the suit plot or for declaration that he was the lawful owner of the suit plot. The High Court Division further found that though the letter of allotment dated 21-3-1962. receipts showing deposit of installments against the suit plot. letter as to the delivery of possession of the suit plot approved plan by RAJUK for construction on the suit plot and the certified copy of the RS khatian Cexhibits- 1. 2 series. 3. 6 and 9 respectively) produced by defendant No.3 resemble or appear to be true are not genuine or true but he managed to get those documents which apparently appear to be original documents just to claim the suit property. In this appeal, the High Court Division also considered the fact that though plaintiff, Shamsul Kabir Humayun Reza (of Title Suit No.176 of 2004) claimed to have filed application before the Parliamentary Standing Committee, such application was neither exhibited nor filed and that "the file which according to him was opened was not called for." The High Court Division further observed that though defendant No.3 claimed that he entered into an agreement with MNH Bulu to sell the suit plot for which he applied to RAJUK for sale permission, but Bulu was not examined. The High Court Division further found that the plaintiffs' father, Shamsul Kabir Humayun Reza, was the lessee of RAJUK and till his death, he exercised his right, title and interest over the suit plot to the knowledge of all. Exhibit-14 dated 7-4-1962 showed that possession of the suit plot was handed over to the father of the plaintiffs. The High Court Division separately considered exhibit-12 series, the original allotment letter and original copies of receipts of the payments of installments by the plaintiffs' father to the DIT against the suit plot; exhibit-17 , the loan sanction letter by the Housing Building Finance Corporation and exhbiit-17Ka dated 17-5-1967, the mortgage deed by which the suit plot was mortgaged by the plaintiffs' father with the House Building Finance Corporation in order to obtain loan; exhibits-18, 19, and 20 series which are the tenancy agreements, the gas bills, electricity bills and City Corporation tax receipts and rent receipts respectively and came to the further finding that it was proved "beyond any doubt" that the plaintiffs' father after getting lease of the suit plot from RAJUK had been exercising his continuous and uninterrupted possession therein. The High Court Division further considered exhibits-25 and 26 which proved that the suit plot was converted into a commercial plot from residential one at the instance of the plaintiffs' father, Shamsul Kabir Humayun Reza of 157, Bara Moghbazar; exhibits-27 and 28 which proved that RAJUK accepted the transfer fees for converting the suit plot as a commercial plot and exhibit-29 showed the approval of plan of multi- storied commercial building by Eastern Housing Limited as the attorney of the plaintiffs' father and these documents showed that RAJUK accepted the plaintiffs' father, Shamsul Kabir Humayun Reza as its lessee and did not accept Shamsul Kabir Humayun Reza of Mirpur and the living Shamsul Kabir Humayun Reza (defendant No.3) as the lessee of the suit plot. The High Court Division came to the further finding that other two Shamsul Kabir Humayun Rezas who claimed the suit plot admittedly were not in possession thereof and both of them made agreement for sale of the suit plot "not as admitted lessee of RAJUK because their papers and documents filed in Title Suit No.51 of 2004 and 176 of 2004 never produced before RANK and claimed that they are the allottee of RAJUK." Plaintiff, Shamsul Kabir Humayun Reza was a fake person. These are all findings of facts.

53. Mr Abdul Wadud Bhuiyan and Mr Rokanuddin Mahmud appearing for the respective petitioner (CP Nos.2199 of 2004 and 1672 of 2011), failed to show, with reference to the evidence on record, both oral and documentary that the findings given by the High Court Division concurring with those of trial Court, were not based on correct sifting of the evidence on record or contrary to the evidence on record and that the reasonings given by the High Court Division in affirming those of the trial Court, were not based on sound judicial principle or otherwise fallacious.

54. This suit being a suit for title along with other prayers, we have also perused the evidence on record and found that the findings, given by the High Court Division in concurring with those of the trial Court, based on proper consideration of the evidence on record. The documents filed by the plaintiffs in support of their case that at the time of allottment of the suit plot by the then DIT, their father was the Executive Engineer of the then C&B and that he used to live at 157, Bara Moghbazar and got possession of the suit plot, paid the installments against the suit plot to the then PIT, got the standard lease deed, constructed building on the suit plot by taking 10 a.m. from the House Building Finance Corporation and had been in possession thereof althrough by letting out the same to the various tenants and now they are in possession thereof through their attorney, Eastern Housing Limited, are so interlinked and clockwise that no prudent man can have any difficulty to come to the decision as to the truth of their claim of title and possession in the suit plot. And a single broad fact as admitted by defendant No.3, Shamsul Kabir Humayun Reza in his deposition as PW1 in Title Suit No. 176 of2004 (in this suit, hl1 was plaintiff and has evidence was adopted in Title Suit No.81 of 2006) that he did not go to the suit plot after December, 1972 when his only son and the brother-in-law disappeared, is enough to disbelieve his case that he was the actual allottee of the suit plot and also to conclude that he was trying to be an imposter just to claim the suit plot. In this regard, we are tempted to say that "possession follows title" and the very fact that the plaintiffs filed all the documents of possession, such as: RS Khatian prepared in the name of their father showing his address at 157, Bara Moghbazar, rent receipts, municipal tax receipts, bills of various utility services, the tenancy agreements first one being of the year, 1967, prima-facie, proved their possession in the suit plot, besides other documents already discussed earlier and the oral evidence: It is also necessary to state that defendant No.3 has failed to file any municipal tax receipts and utility services bills in respect of the suit plot to show that he ever asserted his title to the suit plot and possession therein. The trial Court rightly decreed the suit and the High Court Division committed no wrong in affirming the same.

55. Now the common submissions made by the learned Advocate for the petitioners:

"As to the first common submission, the learned presiding Judge proceeded to dispose of the appeals with the adverse view in respect of the documents produced by the respective petitioner before considering the respective case with reference to the evidence on record as is apparent from the observation made by him at the beginning of the judgment to the effect:

"The cases before me are unique cases of forgery, Allottees, 3 in number are of same name, same parentage, similar hand writings with similar apparently same documents but 3 persons claiming the plot of RAJUK of them, one must be genuine with original papers and others are not. I have taken a strong heavy burden to single out that genuine person with original documents."

56. We find no substance. Our reason being that the learned Judge has not pin-pointed his finger to the documents of any particular party. From the findings given in the judgment as noted earlier, it appears that the learned Judge discussed every bit of evidence, both oral and documentary, adduced by the respective party and gave due weight to their evidence and then gave the finding in respect of the merits of the suits. It is also a fact that from the pleadings of the parties in the 3(three) suits, 3(three) Shamsul Kabir Humayun Rezas surfaced as the claimants of the suit plot as its allottees. As already stated hereinbefore, we have also gone through the evidence on record and found the findings given by the learned Judge, based on proper appreciation of the evidence on record.

57. We do not also find any substance in the second common submission. It appears that though no specific issue was framed by the trial Court in terms as formulated by the learned Advocate for the petitioners, it gave specific finding that Shamsul Kabir Humayun Reza of Mirpur was a fake person and the suit plot was not allotted to him and that Shamsul Kabir Humayun Reza of 157, Bara Moghbazar was the actual allottee of the suit plot. The trial Court gave the further finding that Shamsul Kabir Humayun Reza, plaintiff of Title Suit No.176 of 2004 and also defendant No.3 of Title Suit No.81 of 2006 was not the allotted of the suit plot. The High Court Division, being the last Court of fact, on detailed consideration of the evidence on record, adduced by the parties affirmed those findings of the trial Court and gave further specific finding that the plaintiff of Title SuitNo.51 of2004 produced some forged and fabricated deeds and papers; the documents filed by plaintiff, Shamsul Kabir Humayun Reza, were not genuine or true. The High Court Division gave the further finding that plaintiff, Shamsul Kabir Humayun Reza, was also a fake person. The High Court Division found the documents of the plaintiffs of Title Suit No.81 of 2006 genuine. The evidence adduced by the parties, particularly, those of the plaintiffs of Title Suit NO.81 of 2006, were quite sufficient to decide the issues/points and, in fact, both the Courts decided the points/issues as raised and formulated by the learned Advocate for the petitioners. From the findings of the Courts below, it is apparent that none of the Courts below found any difficulty in giving the finding as to who was the actual allottee and whose documents were genuine and whom RAJUK recognized as its allottee, the real issue in the suits. Therefore, the question of sending either the suit or the appeal back on remand as submitted by the learned Advocates, does not also arise at all. In this regard, we like to observe further that a suit or appeal cannot be sent back on remand as a matter of course or at the fancy of the parties, particularly, when the Courts below decided all the points including the one raised by the learned Advocates .

58. The third and last common submission relates as to the adoption of the evidence by defendant Nos. 1 and 3 in Title Suit No.81 of 2006 adduced by them in Title Suit Nos.51 of2004 and 176 of 2004 in support of their respective case as made out in their written statement as defendants in the suit. The learned presiding Judge discarded the point with the finding as under:

"In civil proceeding as per section 101 of the evidence Act the burden to prove the case of a party in a suit is entirely depend upon him at his own risk. If any party is not interested to prove his case, the court cannot make him bound to prove his case. Here in these three suits since the material issue for decision arisen as to the identity of the real Reza and real lessee all the parties of three suits decided to adopt the evidences given as plaintiff in his suit as evidence of other suit wherein he is a defendant. Accordingly on the application of adoption of each party, the deposition of defendant in the other suit with the consent of all the parties So, the consented party now at the appellate stage cannot raise the question of validity or legality of adoption. Moreover section 33 of evidence Act have also empowers the court to adopt the deposition of one suit as of the deposition of other suit on the consent of the parties involved in the suit. On the other hand, if any plaintiff suit is not desire to adopt his and his witnesses' deposition, as position of DWs in other suit wherein he is defendant, but argued the case without giving evidences it would be at his own risk."

The learned second Judge discarded the point with the finding that "It further appears from the record that the defendants by applications dated 30-6- 2009 prayed for 'adoption of their depositions given as PW 1 in title suit Nos.176 of 2004 and 51 of 2004 respectively as deposition of DW in title suit No.81 of 6 at their own risk.

So it is the plaintiffs of TS Nos.51 of 2004 and 176 of 4 by whose instance the adoption has been made. It will not be out of place to mention here that when the plaintiffs themselves applied in court for adoption of their deposition to late proceedings pending before the same court in respect of same suit land. Appellants surely would not be allowed to raise such questions before the appellate court without raising any question in trial and taking any grounds in the memo of appeal."

59. The learned second Judge took the further view that as the respective plaintiff of Title Suit Nos.51 of 2004 and 176 of 2004 failed to prove their case, the provision of section 33 of the Evidence Act had no manner of "application in the instant appeal, in other word, it cannot be said that the trial Court erred in law in dismissing Title Suit Nos.51 of 2004 and 176 of 2004."

60. Before we examine as to whether the learned Judges were correct in taking the above views, in view of the provisions of section 33 of the Evidence Act, the context, in which the trial Court allowed the prayer for adopting the evidence adduced by defendant Nos.1 and 3 in their respective suit, has to be seen. As it appears from the records, three suits were heard by the same Joint District Judge simultaneously. It is true that in 3(three) suits, the plaintiffs were distinct and' separate and the defendants were also not same. But in Title Suit No.81 of 2006, the plaintiffs of the two other suits (Title Suit Nos.51 of 2004 and 176 of 2004) were defendants and the defendants of those two suits were also impleaded as defendants and the questions involved in the suits were virtually the same as have already been discussed earlier in other context.

61. In Title Suit No.81 of 2006, the plaintiffs were the sons and daughters of late Engineer Shamsul Kabir Humayun Reza of 157, Bara Moghbazar, who claimed that their father was the actual allottee of the suit plot. In this suit, MA Hashem, plaintiff of Title Suit No.51 of 2004, was impleaded as defendant No.1 and plaintiff of Title Suit No.176 of 2004, Shamsul Kabir Humayun Reza, was impleaded as defendant No.3 and Shamsul Kabir Humayun Reza of Mirpur with whom defendant No.1, MA Hashem, claimed to have entered into a contract on 29-12-2002 to purchase the suit plot, was impleaded as defendant No.2; MNH Bulu with whom defendant No.3 allegedly entered into a contract to sell the suit plot was impleaded as defendant No.4; RAJUK and its officials were impleaded as defendant Nos.5-8 and Eastern Housing Limited was impleaded as defendant No.9. In this suit, defendant Nos.1 and 3 filed written statement stating similar facts as stated in the respective plaint (the case of the respective plaint and the written statement has been stated in detail while stating case of the parties). Defendant Nos.4 and 5-8 also filed written statement. In Title Suit No.51 of 2004, the plaintiffs of Title Suit No.81 of 2004 were substituted as defendant Nos.1(a) to 1(e) and they filed written statement stating similar facts as stated in the plaint of Title Suit No.81 of 2006. In Title Suit No.176 of 2004 filed by Shamsul Kabir Humayun, only RAJUK and its officials were made parties. From the pleadings of the parties, a common issue emerged that out of 3(three) Shamsul Kabir Humayun Rezas, who was the actual Shamsul Kabir Humayun Reza and who was the actual allottee of the suit plot? The plaintiffs of Title Suit No.81 of 2006 examined 5(five) witnesses in support of the case that their father late Engineer Shamsul Kabir Humayun Reza of 157, Bara Moghbazar was the actual allottee of the suit plot and their father had been in possession thereof and after his death, they inherited the suit plot and they were in possession thereof and that their father did not enter into any contract with MA Hashem, plaintiff of Title Suit No.51 of 2004. Defendant Nos.1 and 3 who were plaintiffs of Title Suit Nos.51 of 2004 and 176 of 2004 respectively cross-examined each and every PWs(Defendant No.2 did not appear and contest the suit. Defendant No.4 though filed written statement ultimately did not show up). Defendant Nos. l and 3 neither examined themselves nor examined any other witness in this suit. However, they examined themselves and examined other witnesses in their respective suit being Title Suit Nos.51 of 2004 and 176 of 2004 respectively. It is also pertinent to note that the plaintiff of Title Suit No.51 of 2004 who was examined as PW1 was cross-examined by the plaintiffs of Title Suit No.81 of 2006 and defendant Nos.5-8 (RAJUK and its officials) and the plaintiff of Title Suit No.176 of 2004 also was cross-examined by the defendants, namely: RAJUK and its officials. On behalf of RAJUK, one of its officers was examined as DWI in Title Suit No.176 of 2004 and he was duly cross-examined by the plaintiff. On 30-6-2009, defendant Nos. l and 3 filed applications in Title Suit No.81 of 2006 to adopt their evidence adduced in their respective suit, which was allowed by the trial Court. Similarly, the plaintiffs of Title Suit No.81 of 2006 filed application in Title Suit No.51 of 2004 to adopt their evidence in the suit which they adduced in Title Suit No.81 of 2006 and the Court allowed the prayer. Similarly RAJUK also filed application in Title Suit Nos.51 of 2004 and 81 of 2006 to adopt its evidence adduced in Title Suit Not No.176 of 2004 and the Court allowed the prayer. It further appears from the record that no objection, whatsoever, was raised by any of the parties in adopting such evidence and the suits were disposed of accordingly. It is true that Title Suit No.81 of 2006 was filed subsequent to Title Suit Nos.51 of 2004 and 176 of 2004, but it was heard simultaneously with the other two suits. However, it may be kept on record that the examination of PW 1 in Title Suit No.51 of 2004 commenced on 2-4-2006 and examination of the witnesses was concluded on 29-5-2009, examination of witness in Title Suit No.176 of 2004 commenced on 13-3-2008 and was concluded on 12-8-2008 and examination of the witnesses in Title Suit No.81 of2006 commenced on 13- 3-2008 and was concluded on 30-6-2009.

<u>62. A reading of section 33 of the Evidence Act shows that the evidence given by a witness in a judicial proceeding, or before any person authorised by law to take it, is relevant for the purpose of proving, in a subsequent judicial proceeding, or in a later stage of the same proceeding, the truth of the fact which it states. But to consider such evidence the conditions, which must be fulfilled, are that the witness is dead or cannot be found, or is incapable of giving evidence, or is kept out of the way by the adverse party, or if his presence cannot be obtained without an amount of delay or expense which, under the circumstances of the case, the Court considers unreasonable; provided that the proceeding was between the same parties or their representatives in interest; that the adverse party in the first proceeding had the right and opportunity to cross-examine; that the questions in issue were substantially the same in the first as in the second proceeding (Explanation given to the section is not relevant to decide the instant petitions, so omitted from being quoted.) One of the stringent conditions to take recourse to the section on the ground of incapability of a witness of giving evidence has to be proved.</u>

63. In support of his contention, Mr Abdul Wadud Bhuiyan has referred to the cases of Ali Haider vs the State (supra) and Allah Ditta vs the State (supra). Those two cases arose out a criminal trial. In the first case, the depositions of some of witnesses recorded by the Committing Magistrate were transferred to the Sessions record without summoning them on the ground that their evidence were of a formal nature and with a view to avoid unnecessary expenses to the State and the order of the learned Sessions Judge was acquiesced in by the learned Counsel for the accused and then the depositions of the witnesses examined during the committal proceeding were read over to the accused and were used against them. In the context, it was held that in a criminal case, the provisions of section 33 of the Evidence Act cannot be waived; the evidence of the witnesses, illegally admitted, must be excluded. It was also held that the defect was not curable under section 537 of the Code of Criminal Procedure. In the second case, the evidence of a witness named Lala examined during the committal proceeding was also transferred to the Sessions case under section 33 of the Evidence Act and order was passed admitting the evidence declaring that Lala's evidence could not be procured "without delay and unnecessary adjournment" without any evidence before the Court that the witness was incapable of giving evidence on the later date. The defence Counsel conceded that he had no objection to take recourse to section 33 of the Act and in those circumstances, it was held that the Court had misapplied section 33 of the Evidence Act. In that case, it was further held that the witness "could not be found" has to be proved by direct evidence of persons having personal knowledge. It was further held that the defence Counsel's consent did not do away with the necessity of the Court being satisfied by proof on the requirements of section 33. In holding so, in the second case, Cornelius, J who wrote the opinion on behalf of the Court, relied on the observations made by the Privy Council in the case of Chainchal Singh vs Emperor, AIR 1946 PC 1 to the effect:

"Where it is desired to have recourse to section 33 of the Evidence Act on the ground that a witness is incapable of giving evidence, that fact must be proved, and proved strictly. It is an elementary right of an accused person that a witness who is to testify against him should give his evidence before the Court trying the case, which then has the opportunity of seeing the witness and observing his demeanor and can thus form a far better opinion as to his reliability than is possible from reading a statement or deposition. It is necessary that provision should be made for exceptional cases where it is impossible for the witness to be before the Court, and it is only by a statutory provision that this can be achieved. But the Court must be careful to see that the conditions on which the statue permits previous evidence given by the witness to be read are strictly proved."

<u>64. In both the cited cases, the witnesses were not examined in the trial Court and it had no advantage to see the demeanour of the witnesses and the accused had no chance to cross-examine them. But in the instant cases, the context as stated earlier is totally different. Defendant Nos.1 and 3 (petitioner of Civil Petition for Leave to Appeal No.1672 of 2011 and 2199 of 2011) by filing applications adopted the evidence adduced by them in the respective suit as plaintiffs after closing the examination of the plaintiffs' witness in Title Suit No.81 of 2006. And in Title Suit No.81 of 2006 in which their evidence was adopted and considered, they had the advantage to cross-examine the PWs. Therefore, the principle of law enunciated in the cases cited by Mr Bhuiyan, has got no manner of application in the instant cases. The depositions of the witnesses-examined in the two suits (Title Suit Nos.51 of 2004 and 176 of 2004) and the documents exhibited therein by the respective plaintiff, are definitely evidence within the meaning of section 3 of the Evidence Act. So, we do not see any bar under section 33 of the Act to consider the evidence adduced in the suits having been heard simultaneously and the common questions of facts and laws were involved. Moreso, the onus to prove the facts as asserted by defendant Nos. 1 and 3 in their respective written statement filed in Title Suit No.81 of 2006, was squarely upon them within the meaning of sections 101 and 102 of the Evidence Act and they opted not to examine any witness in Title Suit No.81 of 2006 in support of their case and adopted the evidence adduced by them in their own suits. In the context, we do not also see any illegality committed by the trial Court in allowing the prayer made by the respective defendant to adopt the evidence adduced in the respective suit and such a course, in no way, caused any prejudice to them. Therefore, the trial court did not commit any illegality in considering the evidence of the respective defendant adduced in the respective suit as plaintiff within the meaning of section 33 of the Evidence Act in coming to the finding as to the proof and disproof of facts involved in the suits and thus deciding their merits. In this regard, we consider it necessary to state the law that in the Code of Civil Procedure, there is no specific provision to allow a party in a suit or a civil proceeding to adopt evidence adduced by him in another suit or proceeding heard simultaneously or analogously, but section 151 of the Code has given inherent power to the Court to pass any such order to secure the ends of justice and in the instant cases by allowing the prayer of the defendants to adopt their evidence given as plaintiff in their respective suit in Title Suit No.81 of 2006, ends of justice has been served and that also prevented the abuse of the process of the Court. It is also a fact that the petitioners herein did not raise any objection, whatsoever, before the trial Court against the adoption of their evidence in their respective suit as a defence in Title Suit No.81 of 2006 and allowed the suits to be disposed accordingly, and the petitioners did not also take any ground in the Memorandum of Appeal in that respect as observed by the High Court Division. A party should not be allowed to change its position as per its own sweet will and cannot be allowed to make a summer sault or make a 'U' turn after the disposal of the suit and that there was no allegation of fraud as well. It appears to us that objection as to the adoption of the evidence given in Title Suit Nos.51 of 2004 and 176 of 2004, in Title Suit No.81 of 2006 citing section 33 of the Evidence Act was taken before the High Court Division for the first time during the hearing of the appeal just to delay the disposal of the suits by way of sending them back on remand to the trial Court giving them chance to adduce evidence,</u>

65. In view of the above, we find no merits in the last common submission of the learned Advocates. And, yet we are to address another objection raised by the learned Advocates for the petitioners as to observations/comments and remarks made by the learned presiding Judge in his judgment with which the learned second Judge also differed.

66. The learned presiding Judge after considering the merits of the respective appeal with reference to the pleadings and the evidence on record and giving his findings thereon made the following observations/ comments and remarks:

"Before lifting my pen I put a question to myself as to what is our destiny. Lord Macauley said before leaving for India for making laws stated that chieknery (sic), purgery and forgery are basic character of the inhabitants of India about 200 years before. I feel it is still continuing. 'For a piece of land all these people even changes their parentage, for a post we superseed(sic) our friend by selling our head and for grabbing anything we tell lie off and on. Leadership start(sic) with a small lie in family. It increases with the advancement of leadership i.e. from own home to national. Every leader's hands are smeared with bloods. If the leaders could realise how much blood is required to make them leader then they might not tell lie and might not have done mischiefs to the nation which they are doing. I can rest a sure that except a few most of the leaders have no source of income. If head of institute sells his/her head then what remain. The person who is the beneficieny (sic) of any wrong in open breast shouted for doing good, doing justice and to prevent corruption. Person sub-merged in corruption shouted for preventing corruption. After getting a so called independency judiciary becomes more dependent than its earlier days. Nowadays we are deciding disputed political issues without considering these are the business of Parliament which represents the entire nation and its citizens. We are pleasing a master at the expense of the institution. What a funny. Our National Poet Kazi Nazrul Islam in one of his verse wrote: চাপরাশীর ঐ তকমার চেয়ে তলোয়ারে বড় যেন’ and in other place ‘যাহা চাই যেন জয় করে পাই গ্রহণ না করি দান’ do not know how the future generation will judge us and what they will think about us."

<u>67. These observations/comments and remarks of the learned presiding Judge were absolutely unnecessary and uncalled for as those were beyond the issues involved in the appeals. After the learned Judge had come to the conclusions of his findings as to the merit of the respective appeal, he should have stopped there. A Judge should not be emotional and should not embark upon making unnecessary observations/comments and remarks, such observations /comments and remarks may give bad message to the litigants and the public, at large, as to the independence of judiciary and dispensation of even handed justice by it. While disposing of a matter, a Judge must confine himself to the pleadings of the parties and answer the issues which would emerge from the pleadings in the light of the evidence adduced and materials placed before it and the law governing the field. A Judge must refrain himself from making unnecessary observations/comments and remarks as have been made in the instant cases. In the context, the observations /comments and remarks as quoted hereinbefore cannot remain as part of the judgment and accordingly, those are expunged.</u>

68. Be that as it may, in view of the findings given by the High Court Division on the issues involved in the suits concurring with those of the trial Court as discussed hereinabove, we maintain the direction given by the learned presiding Judge to the effect "I am directing Registrar of this Court to initiate a case as per Section 476 of the Code of Criminal Procedure before the appropriate Court immediately against the fictitious Shamsul Kabir Humayun Reza and MA Hashem for using false documents before Courts. I (sic) further directing the Registrar of this Court to send a copy of the judgment to the Chairman, RAJUK so that he shall make inquiry for sliping so many apparently original documents/deeds from the custody of DIT and start criminal case against those persons who are involved in forging all these documents, Dhaka Improvement Trust now RAJUK is custodian of Dhaka but their activities shows(sic) that they are not reliable persons. As soon as possible it trace (sic) out the culprits of RAJUK and act in accordance with law will better for the inhabitants of Dhaka City."

69. We find no merits in the petitions. Accordingly, the petitions are dismissed.

End.
